// momoed.momo - the editor (§55).
//
// A file named on the command line - or none, and the explorer says what is here
// - with §54's buffer under it, §56's window onto it, §59 searching it, §60
// holding the term, §61 moving spans of it about, §62 listing the directory,
// §67 driving the menus, §57's keys driving all of them, and cells written
// straight to the text frame.
//
// **Almost nothing here is new.** The buffer, the window, the search, the field,
// the spans and the keyboard are libraries with test projects of their own, and
// `edloop`, `edfind` and `edsel` drive this exact command layer from a script.
// What this file adds is the three things no headless tier can run: a real
// keyboard, a real screen, and a real file named by a person.
//
// It has a `.expected` covering less than it looks: run as `momoed /?` it prints
// a usage line and stops, so tier 2 reaches the command tail and the exit and
// nothing past them. Everything else here is tested in the libraries below,
// which is why it was built in that order.

include "lib/std/key.momo"
include "lib/std/str.momo"
include "lib/std/screen.momo"
include "lib/std/mode.momo"
include "lib/moview.momo"
include "lib/mofind.momo"
include "lib/mofield.momo"
include "lib/morange.momo"
include "lib/modir.momo"
include "lib/momenu.momo"

// The frame is declared for the largest text mode this supports, because a `far`
// region takes a constant size (§16) while the geometry is read back from the
// BIOS (§43) - 80x43 on EGA and 80x50 on VGA are the same pair of calls, and
// only the hardware knows which happened. So the declaration is the ceiling and
// `screenWidth` and `screenHeight` are the truth, clamped to it.
const edMaxCols = 80
const edMaxRows = 50

// **The frame's segment is a variable here**, which §43's rule says it should not
// be - and that rule is priced for `tigerpic` writing 92,949 pixels, not for an
// editor writing 1,920 cells a few times a second. §16 reloads ES per access
// either way; what a variable adds is `mov dx, [seg]` where a constant had
// `mov dx, imm`, and `drawrate` is how that got priced rather than argued.
//
// What it buys is mode 7, whose frame is at 0xB000 - the screen `mode mono`
// leaves, which `edit.com` works in and this refused. A refusal was the honest
// answer while the segment was nailed down; it is not an answer now.
u16 vramSeg

far u16[edMaxCols * edMaxRows] vram = vramSeg

// **An attribute is a property of the adapter, not a constant.** MDA has no
// colours at all: 0x07 is normal, 0x0F bright, 0x70 reverse and 0x01
// underline, and a byte meaning white-on-blue means none of those. The two
// sets agree on the two bytes that happen to mean the same thing either way,
// which is why the status line looked right in mono even when nothing else did.
u8 attrText
u8 attrStatus
u8 attrMatch
u8 attrSel

// **The letter an `Alt` shortcut uses, and `vidprobe` says it is one byte in
// both schemes** - which is not what the design expected. Underline was the plan,
// being the classic mode-7 attribute, and it does not render on this hardware at
// all: `0x01` is indistinguishable from `0x07`. What does render is the
// intensity bit over a white background.
//
// So this joins `0x07` and `0x70` as a third byte the two schemes agree on -
// white on light grey to a VGA, bright white on white to mode 7 - and every
// attribute the chrome needs is now in the set that needs no translation.
u8 attrKey

// The `Alt` letter on the *open* menu's title, where the row is normal video and
// `attrKey`'s white-on-grey would paint a grey block over it. Bright on the text
// background instead - which is `0x0F` in both schemes, a fourth byte they agree
// on after `0x07`, `0x70` and `0x7F`, and so a const rather than a fifth line in
// each of `useColor` and `useMono`.
const attrKeyOn = 0x0F

sub useColor {
  attrText   = color( lightGray, black )
  attrStatus = color( black, lightGray )
  attrMatch  = color( black, cyan )
  attrSel    = color( white, blue )
  attrKey    = color( white, lightGray )
}

sub useMono {
  attrText   = 0x07
  attrStatus = 0x70
  attrMatch  = 0x0F
  attrSel    = 0x70

  // Measured rather than chosen: every candidate carrying the intensity bit
  // stood out on a reverse bar and every candidate without it either vanished
  // into the bar or matched it. `attrSel` is still `attrStatus` here - mode 7
  // has one more state than this uses and it is deliberately not spent on
  // separating them, because a whole selected region in bright-on-white is
  // legible but low contrast, and the two are adjacent at one row boundary.
  attrKey    = 0x7F
}

u16 cols
u16 rows

// ---- the explorer ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// VS Code's shape rather than a file manager's: it shows the directory the
// editor was opened in and does not navigate, so there is no `.`, no `..` and
// nothing to get lost in. `^O` still takes a path, which is what covers the
// case this deliberately does not.
//
// **Fourteen columns, derived rather than picked.** A DOS name is 8.3 and so
// cannot exceed twelve characters - unlike VS Code, where a name is any length -
// so a wider panel is permanent blank. One column for a marker, twelve for the
// name, one for the scrollbar, which doubles as the separator:
//
//     * MOMOED  MOMO|
//
// That leaves 66 columns of editor at 80, where 20 would leave 60. The source
// this is used on is 80 columns wide and neither fits, so the six matter.
const expCols = 14
const expName = 12

bool expShown
bool expFocus
bool needExp

// The directory half of what was named on the command line - everything up to
// and including the last separator, which is empty for a bare filename. The
// pattern is that plus `*.*`, and an entry is opened by putting a name back on
// the end of it.
u8[80] dirPath
u16 dirPathLen
u8[80] dirPat
u8[80] openPath

// Where the editor starts and how wide it is, which is the one thing showing the
// panel changes about everything else.
u16 edLeft()  => expShown ? expCols : 0
u16 edWidth() => expShown ? cols - expCols : cols

// ---- two rows of chrome above the text (§66) --------------------------------
//
// A menu bar and a tab bar, both always on screen. `edit.com` on the same machine
// spends more - a menu row, a title row, a horizontal scrollbar and a status row,
// plus a left border and a vertical scrollbar in the columns - so 22 text rows of
// 25 is not the cost it first looks like.
//
// **Always visible rather than appearing with a second file.** A bar that comes
// and goes is worse than a row, and the tab bar is the title bar as well.
//
// These two are the whole of the change: every routine that measured from row
// zero or from `rows - 1` measures from here instead.
const edChrome = 2

u16 edTop()    => edChrome
u16 edHeight() => rows - 1 - edChrome

// The name being edited, and the one to go back to when an open is refused.
const nameMax = 80

u8[nameMax] fileName
u8[nameMax] wasName

// **Where a Save As name waits for an answer.** It used to go straight into
// `fileName`, which meant Cancel left the document wearing a name nothing on disk
// had - and one `^S` from overwriting the file it had just declined to.
u8[nameMax] saveAsName
bool haveName
bool dirty

// ---- several files open at once (§65) ---------------------------------------
//
// §54 holds the buffers and §56 holds a window onto each, so what is left here is
// the half neither library can know: the name, whether there is one, and whether
// it has been changed since it was read.
//
// **Saved and reloaded around a switch** rather than indexed at every use, which
// is §54's own shape one layer down and keeps `fileName` the single thing the
// rest of this file reads. Two hundred and forty bytes to avoid touching every
// reference to a name in the program.
//
// `docOpen` is a flag per slot rather than a count, because closing the *first*
// of two leaves the second one open and a count cannot say that.
const edMaxDocs = viewMaxDocs

u8[edMaxDocs * nameMax] docNames
u8[edMaxDocs] docHave
u8[edMaxDocs] docDirty
u8[edMaxDocs] docOpen

// The explorer rides in the cycle as one more slot past the documents, which is
// what `Ctrl+Tab` reaching it costs: one index, no second key to learn and no
// second mechanism. It is a little impure - it is a pane and not a tab - and the
// alternative was a third shortcut for a screen with two panes on it.
const expSlot   = edMaxDocs
const cycleSize = expSlot + 1

// `/?` or `-?`, DOS's convention. **It is also the only thing tier 2 can reach
// in this program**: every other path opens a screen and waits for a key, and
// removing the old usage line - which no-arguments used to print - would have
// taken the whole project out of the e2e tier with it. A help flag is worth
// having on its own and costs the coverage nothing.
bool helping

// Whether a load may print. True at startup, where the DOS prompt is still on
// screen and there is nothing else to look at; false for `^O`, where the
// editor is drawn and dots would land in the middle of the document.
bool loud

// **The prompt serves five questions and is one mechanism.** What differs is the
// label, what Enter does, and whether Enter closes it - three facts, held here
// rather than in five copies of the same loop.
//
// Replace is two of these in a row, which is the case that decided the shape: a
// command can answer Enter by asking the *next* question, and then a prompt that
// knows what it is for is all the machinery a two-part command needs.
const askNone = 0
const askFind = 1
const askGoto = 2
const askOpen = 3
const askTerm = 4
const askWith = 5
const askSave = 6

u8  asking
u16 askLabel

// Where the field starts, **measured from the label rather than written down**.
// It used to be a const that had to agree with the width of one string; with
// five strings that is four more chances to be a column out, and the caret
// sitting beside what it is editing is exactly the kind of wrong that gets
// noticed last.
u16 askAt

// `message` is an address of a `$` string or 0, shown beside whichever status
// line is up and cleared by the next key - so a "not found" is visible until the
// person does something and then is not.
u16 message

// Whether the last search in this prompt landed on something, which is the one
// thing the renderer needs that §59 cannot answer on its own - `findLine` and
// `findCol` still hold the previous match after a failed one.
bool onMatch

bool prompting() => asking != askNone

// The replacement text, kept out of the field because the field is about to be
// the second question.
u8[fieldMax] replText
u16 replLen

// What the last answer parsed to, and what the last replace did.
u16 askValue
u16 askCount

// **Four kilobytes rather than 128**, which was the size when the heap had
// nothing spare and is a DOS call per 128 bytes of a file. On a floppy a quarter
// of a megabyte that way is a lot of calls, and it is also what makes a progress
// dot per read a sensible unit: fifty-odd dots for a big file, not two thousand.
const pieceSize = 4096
view u8[pieceSize] piece = _heap[dirHeap]

// The clipboard, after §54's claim and after the read buffer - §17 static
// partitioning, with this program's two pieces going after the library's one.
// **It is where the 64 KB starts to bite**: `npm run memory` is what says how
// much is left, and PLAN §58 is where the ceiling goes up.
const clipBytes = 8192
view u8[clipBytes] clip = _heap[dirHeap + pieceSize]
u16 clipLen

// Whether the keystroke being handled had Shift on it. §57 folds Shift into the
// key space and `step` folds it back out, so this is what is left of it by the
// time a motion runs.
bool selecting

// The span `^C` and `^X` are about to act on, worked out once by `actSpan` so
// that the copy and the delete cannot disagree about it.
u16 spanFromLine
u16 spanFromCol
u16 spanToLine
u16 spanToCol

const u8[] sNoFile  = " (new)$"
const u8[] sSaved   = " saved$"
const u8[] sFailed  = "WRITE FAILED - file damaged$"
const u8[] sTooBig  = "file does not fit - not opened$"
const u8[] sNoLines = "too many lines$"
const u8[] sNoChunk = "too much text$"
const u8[] sNoMem   = "not enough memory past the program$"
const u8[] sOf      = " of $"
const u8[] sLoading = "loading $"
const u8[] sLines   = " lines, room for $"
const u8[] sChunks  = " chunks of text$"
const u8[] sNoRoom  = " FULL$"
const u8[] sFind    = "find: $"
const u8[] sGoto    = "go to line: $"
const u8[] sOpen    = "open: $"
const u8[] sTerm    = "replace: $"
const u8[] sWith    = "with: $"
const u8[] sDirty   = "unsaved changes - save first$"
const u8[] sNoLine  = "no such line$"
const u8[] sDone    = "replaced $"
const u8[] sNoMatch = "not found$"
const u8[] sWrapped = "wrapped$"
const u8[] sNoUndo  = "too big to undo$"
const u8[] sClipFull = "clipboard full$"
const u8[] sUsage    = "usage: momoed [FILE]  -  no FILE opens the explorer here$"
const u8[] sSave     = "save as: $"
const u8[] sNoName   = "(untitled)$"
const u8[] sTooMany  = "too many files$"
const u8[] sKb       = "K$"
const u8[] sHere     = ".$"
const u8[] sNoSlots  = "no free tab - close one first$"
const u8[] sOneDoc   = "this is the only file open$"
const u8[][] sButtons   = [ "Yes$", "No$", "Cancel$" ]
const u8[] sSaveChanges = "Save changes to$"
const u8[] sOverwrite   = "Replace the existing file$"

const u8[] sPickTitle = " pick a character $"
const u8[] sPickKeys  = " Enter inserts  Esc cancels $"
const u8[] sPickDec   = "dec $"
const u8[] sPickHex   = "  hex $"
const u8[] sPickAlt   = "  Alt+$"
const u8[] sPickEol   = "  no - ends a line$"
const u8[] sPickSmall = "screen too small for the picker$"


// ---- the command tail ----

// DOS leaves it at PSP:0080h - a length byte, the text, then a carriage return -
// and `DS = CS` with the PSP as the first 256 bytes of our own segment, so this
// is an ordinary `peek` (§10) and needs nothing at all.
//
// §38 notes the collision this sits beside: `FindFirst` writes to the DTA, which
// defaults to these same bytes. Nothing here enumerates a directory, and the
// explorer that will is what has to move the DTA first.
bool takeName() {
  u16 n
  u16 at
  u16 out

  n = peek8( 0x80 )
  at = 0x81
  out = 0

  while ( n > 0 && peek8( at ) == ' ' ) {
    at++
    n--
  }

  while ( n > 0 && peek8( at ) != ' ' && peek8( at ) != 13 && out < len( fileName ) - 1 ) {
    fileName[out] = peek8( at )
    out++
    at++
    n--
  }

  fileName[out] = 0

  // **The directory half, for the explorer.** Everything up to and including the
  // last separator, which is nothing at all for a bare filename - so `momoed x.c`
  // lists the current directory and `momoed sub\x.c` lists `sub`. A drive letter
  // counts as a separator: `d:x.c` is a name on `d:`, not a name containing a
  // colon.
  //
  // 92 rather than an escape, because a backslash inside a character literal is
  // one more thing to get right in a line nobody will read twice.
  dirPathLen = 0
  for ( u16 i = 0; fileName[i] != 0; i++ ) {
    if ( fileName[i] == 92 || fileName[i] == 58 ) dirPathLen = i + 1
  }

  memCopy( addr( dirPath ), addr( fileName ), dirPathLen )

  return out > 0
}

// The directory plus `*.*`. Built once and rebuilt by nothing, because this
// editor does not change directory - which is the whole of the decision that
// there is no `..` in the panel.
sub buildPattern {
  memCopy( addr( dirPat ), addr( dirPath ), dirPathLen )

  dirPat[dirPathLen]     = '*'
  dirPat[dirPathLen + 1] = '.'
  dirPat[dirPathLen + 2] = '*'
  dirPat[dirPathLen + 3] = 0
}

// A name from the panel put back on the end of the directory, so what reaches
// `fileOpen` is a path and not a name that happens to work from here. Returns
// the length, because the caller needs it and counting it twice is how the two
// answers come to differ.
u16 buildOpenPath( u16 nameAt ) {
  u16 n

  memCopy( addr( openPath ), addr( dirPath ), dirPathLen )

  n = 0
  while ( peek8( nameAt + n ) != 0 ) {
    openPath[ dirPathLen + n ] = peek8( nameAt + n )
    n++
  }

  openPath[ dirPathLen + n ] = 0
  return dirPathLen + n
}

// ---- the file ----

// A file that is not there is not an error: it is a new file, which is what an
// editor is for. Only a file that is there and will not open is worth refusing.
bool loadFile() {
  u16 handle
  u16 got
  u16 kb

  // **`textClear`, not `textInit`.** §54 is explicit about the difference and it
  // only started to matter when there was a second document to lose: `textInit`
  // lays out the machine for every slot, which an open of one file must not do.
  textClear()

  handle = fileOpen( addr( fileName ), fileReadOnly )
  if ( fileFailed() ) return true

  got = fileRead( handle, addr( piece ), pieceSize )
  kb = 0

  // **The whole read is one `textBulk`**, which is where §54 keeps the append
  // position in ordinary variables instead of walking to it a character at a
  // time. Measured at four times on the same bytes, and it is the difference
  // between a file open that is loop-bound and one that is not.
  //
  // Reading the file and printing a dot are both fine in here: the rule is that
  // nothing may read the *buffer*, and neither of these is the buffer.
  textBulk {
    while ( got > 0 ) {
      // A dot a read, which is the only thing on screen for the length of a
      // floppy. Three quarters of a minute of nothing reads as a machine that
      // has stopped, and `edit.com` spends the same time saying how far along
      // it is.
      if ( loud ) {
        putChar( '.' )
      } else {
        showLoading( kb )
      }

      kb = kb + got / 1024

      for ( u16 i = 0; i < got; i++ ) {
        textLoad( piece[i] )
      }

      // **Stop at the first refusal rather than reading to the end.** §54
      // declines every byte after the buffer is full, so carrying on is a
      // quarter of a megabyte of floppy read to arrive at an answer that was
      // known three minutes earlier.
      if ( textNoRoom() ) break

      got = fileRead( handle, addr( piece ), pieceSize )
    }
  }

  fileClose( handle )

  // A file too big for the buffer loads part way and stops, and every refusal
  // inside §54 leaves the buffer unchanged rather than half changed - which is
  // right, and is also silent. Saving that back would write the truncation over
  // the original, so this is the one failure the editor must not paper over.
  return !textNoRoom()
}

// **A save that could not finish must not be reported as one.** `fileCreate`
// truncates before a byte is written, so a failure here leaves the file on disk
// shorter than the document - and the one thing that must not then happen is
// `dirty` being cleared, because the copy in memory is the only correct one left.
//
// Three things can go wrong and only the first used to be looked at.
bool saveFile() {
  u16 handle
  bool ok

  handle = fileCreate( addr( fileName ) )
  if ( fileFailed() ) return false

  ok = textSave( handle )

  // **The close is a write too.** DOS flushes the last partial sector at
  // `AH=3Eh`, so a disk that fills on the final buffer fails here and nowhere
  // earlier - which is the one failure that would still have been invisible with
  // every `fileWrite` checked.
  fileClose( handle )
  if ( fileFailed() ) return false

  return ok
}

// Which of the three limits, and what the machine had. **A file that ran out of
// lines and one that ran out of text want different numbers raised**, and the
// message that said neither had been the wrong half of the answer twice.
u16 whyNotOpened() {
  if ( textWhyNoRoom() == whyLines ) return addr( sNoLines )
  if ( textWhyNoRoom() == whyChunks ) return addr( sNoChunk )
  if ( textWhyNoRoom() == whyBlock ) return addr( sNoMem )

  return addr( sTooBig )
}

// ---- the screen ----

sub cellAt( u16 x, u16 y, u8 ch, u8 attr ) {
  vram[ y * cols + x ] = u16( ch ) | ( u16( attr ) << 8 )
}

// ---- one row, composed before any of it is written (§68) ---------------------
//
// **The flicker was blank-then-draw**, not redrawing too often. Every routine
// that filled a row with spaces and then wrote content over the top was writing
// each cell twice a frame with a blank first, and whatever the beam caught
// between the two writes was that blank. A cell written once with its final
// value does not flicker however often it is rewritten.
//
// `drawExplorer` was the proof and it was already here: it writes each cell
// exactly once and is the one piece of chrome nobody reported flickering, on the
// busiest redraw path there is.
//
// This is that rule made structural rather than maintained. Five routines placed
// text at computed columns over a row somebody else had cleared, which is an
// arrangement where writing once is nobody's job; composing in near memory and
// blitting a span makes it fall out instead.
//
// It is also **fewer far writes**, not more: the status line paid eighty blanks
// a frame before anything was drawn on it.
u16[edMaxCols] rowBuf

sub rowFill( u16 from, u16 to, u8 ch, u8 attr ) {
  for ( u16 x = from; x < to && x < cols; x++ ) {
    rowBuf[x] = u16( ch ) | ( u16( attr ) << 8 )
  }
}

sub rowPut( u16 x, u8 ch, u8 attr ) {
  if ( x < cols ) rowBuf[x] = u16( ch ) | ( u16( attr ) << 8 )
}

// `stop` rather than the screen edge, because a row has more than one thing on
// it and they are not all at the left - which is the clipping `statusClip` was
// written for and is now the only place it lives.
sub rowStr( u16 x, u16 at, u16 stop, u8 attr ) {
  u16 i
  i = 0

  while ( peek8( at + i ) != '$' && x + i < stop ) {
    rowPut( x + i, peek8( at + i ), attr )
    i++
  }
}

sub rowBlit( u16 y, u16 from, u16 to ) {
  u16 base
  base = y * cols

  for ( u16 x = from; x < to && x < cols; x++ ) {
    vram[ base + x ] = rowBuf[x]
  }
}

// The routine §56 calls, and the only thing that differs between this and the
// test projects that drive the same library. The row is padded to the full width
// rather than left short, because what is past the text is last frame's pixels
// until something writes over them.
// **The overlays are worked out before the row is written, not painted over it.**
// §68's rule, and this is the routine where it mattered least to a person and
// most to the shape of the rule: a selected cell was written once in `attrText`
// and again in `attrSel`, which flashes a colour rather than a blank and so goes
// unnoticed - and an exception here is how the rule would have died.
//
// **The plain path is untouched.** A row with neither a selection nor a match on
// it runs the two loops it has always run, because that is the scroll path and
// `drawrate` is what says the difference matters. Everything below the guard is
// new and costs nothing to the rows that do not take it.
sub viewRow( u16 y, u16 at, u16 n ) {
  u16 base
  u16 hi
  u16 ln
  u16 c0
  u16 c1
  u16 w
  u16 selFrom
  u16 selTo
  u16 mFrom
  u16 mTo
  u8  ch
  u8  a

  base = ( edTop() + y ) * cols + edLeft()
  w = edWidth()

  // The attribute used to be a `const` and folded into the instruction. It is
  // the adapter's now, so it is composed once for the row rather than read per
  // cell - which is where a variable would have cost something.
  hi = u16( attrText ) << 8

  // The selection as a span of *window* columns, which is the conversion the old
  // painting loop did one cell at a time. §56 answers which end comes first, so
  // nothing here has to know that a selection made upwards has its anchor after
  // its cursor. Empty when this row has none, which is what the guard reads.
  selFrom = 0
  selTo   = 0

  if ( viewSelected() ) {
    ln = viewTop() + y

    if ( ln >= viewFromLine() && ln <= viewToLine() ) {
      c0 = 0
      if ( ln == viewFromLine() ) c0 = viewFromCol()

      c1 = lineLength( ln )
      if ( ln == viewToLine() ) {
        c1 = viewToCol()
      } else {
        // One past the text, which is the newline. Without it a blank line in
        // the middle of a selection is the one row that looks unselected.
        c1++
      }

      if ( c0 < viewLeft() ) c0 = viewLeft()
      if ( c1 > viewLeft() + w ) c1 = viewLeft() + w

      if ( c1 > c0 ) {
        selFrom = c0 - viewLeft()
        selTo   = c1 - viewLeft()
      }
    }
  }

  // The match is marked while the prompt is open, and only then. The caret is
  // down in the status line for as long as a person is typing a term, so
  // without this a search says which *line* it found and leaves them to work
  // out which word - which is most of the answer missing on a line with two.
  //
  // Clipped to `n` rather than to the width, because past the end of the row
  // there is nothing of the match left on screen.
  mFrom = 0
  mTo   = 0

  if ( prompting() && onMatch && viewTop() + y == findLine() ) {
    c0 = findCol()
    c1 = findCol() + findLength()

    if ( c0 < viewLeft() ) c0 = viewLeft()
    if ( c1 > viewLeft() + n ) c1 = viewLeft() + n

    if ( c1 > c0 ) {
      mFrom = c0 - viewLeft()
      mTo   = c1 - viewLeft()
    }
  }

  if ( selTo == selFrom && mTo == mFrom ) {
    for ( u16 x = 0; x < n; x++ ) {
      vram[ base + x ] = u16( peek8( at + x ) ) | hi
    }

    for ( u16 x = n; x < w; x++ ) {
      vram[ base + x ] = 32 | hi
    }

    return
  }

  for ( u16 x = 0; x < w; x++ ) {
    ch = 32
    if ( x < n ) ch = peek8( at + x )

    a = attrText
    if ( x >= selFrom && x < selTo ) a = attrSel
    if ( x >= mFrom && x < mTo ) a = attrMatch

    vram[ base + x ] = u16( ch ) | ( u16( a ) << 8 )
  }
}

// ---- the menu bar and the tab bar -------------------------------------------

sub drawMenus {
  u16 x
  u16 at
  bool lit

  rowFill( 0, cols, ' ', attrStatus )

  for ( u16 m = 0; m < menuCount(); m++ ) {
    at  = menuTitle( m )
    x   = menuBarX( m )
    lit = menuUp() && menuAt() == m

    // The open menu's title is a hole in the bar, which is the tab bar's rule and
    // the explorer's before it - and it joins up with the box hanging below it.
    rowStr( x, at, cols, lit ? attrText : attrStatus )
    rowPut( x, peek8( at ), lit ? attrKeyOn : attrKey )
  }

  rowBlit( 0, 0, cols )
}

// ---- a question with three answers (§69) -------------------------------------
//
// `^Q` used to refuse and ask for a second press, which is `^O`'s and `^W`'s rule
// and was right for a key. **It does not survive being a menu item**: reaching
// File > Exit a second time means opening the menu again, and opening a menu is a
// keystroke, which disarmed the door. So Exit could never get through.
//
// Three answers rather than two, and the third is the one a double-tap could not
// have: *save and go*, *go anyway* and *stay* are three intentions, and two of
// them were the same gesture at different speeds.
//
// **It is in the program, where §64's picker is and §67's menus are not.** The
// state is three buttons and a reason code; what is interesting is what each
// answer *does*, and that is entirely this program's and could not be tested
// headless anyway.
const dlgNone      = 0
const dlgExit      = 1
const dlgOverwrite = 2

u8  dlgWhy
u16 dlgQuestion
u16 dlgSubject
u16 dlgPick

bool dlgUp() => dlgWhy != dlgNone

u16 dlgCount() => len( sButtons )
u16 dlgButton( u16 i ) => addr( sButtons[i] )

u8 fold( u8 c ) => c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' ? c - 32 : c

// One space either side of each label so the focused one reads as a block, and
// two between - measured rather than written down, so a renamed button moves the
// row it is in.
const dlgGap = 2

u16 dlgRowW() {
  u16 w
  w = 0

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < dlgCount(); i++ ) {
    w = w + strLen( dlgButton( i ) ) + 2
    if ( i + 1 < dlgCount() ) w = w + dlgGap
  }

  return w
}

// ASCIZ, because the only two subjects there are are filenames.
u16 dlgSubjectLen() {
  u16 n
  n = 0

  while ( peek8( dlgSubject + n ) != 0 ) {
    n++
  }

  return n
}

u16 dlgW() {
  u16 w

  w = dlgRowW()
  if ( strLen( dlgQuestion ) > w ) w = strLen( dlgQuestion )
  if ( dlgSubjectLen() > w ) w = dlgSubjectLen()

  w = w + 4
  if ( w > cols ) w = cols

  return w
}

const dlgH = 6

u16 dlgX() => ( cols - dlgW() ) / 2
u16 dlgY() => ( rows - dlgH ) / 2

// Where each button's label starts, so the drawing and the cursor agree without
// either of them counting twice.
u16 dlgButtonX( u16 i ) {
  u16 x
  x = dlgX() + ( dlgW() - dlgRowW() ) / 2 + 1

  for ( u16 j = 0; j < i; j++ ) {
    x = x + strLen( dlgButton( j ) ) + 2 + dlgGap
  }

  return x
}

sub dlgOpen( u8 why, u16 question, u16 subject ) {
  message     = 0
  dlgWhy      = why
  dlgQuestion = question
  dlgSubject  = subject
  dlgPick     = 0
}

// ---- drawing it ----

sub dlgEdge( u16 y, u8 left, u8 right ) {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw

  bx = dlgX()
  bw = dlgW()

  rowPut( bx, left, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < bw - 1; x++ ) {
    rowPut( bx + x, boxH, attrStatus )
  }

  rowPut( bx + bw - 1, right, attrStatus )
  rowBlit( y, bx, bx + bw )
}

sub dlgBlank {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw

  bx = dlgX()
  bw = dlgW()

  rowPut( bx, boxV, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < bw - 1; x++ ) {
    rowPut( bx + x, ' ', attrStatus )
  }

  rowPut( bx + bw - 1, boxV, attrStatus )
}

// **The focused button is a hole in the box**, which is the explorer's trick, the
// tab bar's, the menu's and the picker's. Five places now, one rule, and it is
// the rule that costs no attribute - which is what mode 7 could not have spared.
sub dlgButtons {
  u16 x
  u16 at
  u16 n
  u8  a

  dlgBlank()

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < dlgCount(); i++ ) {
    a = attrStatus
    if ( i == dlgPick ) a = attrText

    at = dlgButton( i )
    n  = strLen( at )
    x  = dlgButtonX( i )

    rowPut( x - 1, ' ', a )
    rowStr( x, at, dlgX() + dlgW() - 1, a )
    rowPut( x + n, ' ', a )
  }

  rowBlit( dlgY() + 4, dlgX(), dlgX() + dlgW() )
}

sub dlgText( u16 y, u16 at ) {
  dlgBlank()
  rowStr( dlgX() + 2, at, dlgX() + dlgW() - 1, attrStatus )
  rowBlit( y, dlgX(), dlgX() + dlgW() )
}

sub drawDialog {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw
  u16 i

  dlgEdge( dlgY(), boxTL, boxTR )

  dlgText( dlgY() + 1, dlgQuestion )

  // The subject is ASCIZ where everything else on a row is `$`-terminated, so it
  // is placed rather than passed to `rowStr`.
  bx = dlgX()
  bw = dlgW()

  dlgBlank()
  i = 0

  while ( peek8( dlgSubject + i ) != 0 && bx + 2 + i < bx + bw - 1 ) {
    rowPut( bx + 2 + i, peek8( dlgSubject + i ), attrStatus )
    i++
  }

  rowBlit( dlgY() + 2, bx, bx + bw )

  dlgBlank()
  rowBlit( dlgY() + 3, bx, bx + bw )

  dlgButtons()

  dlgEdge( dlgY() + 5, boxBL, boxBR )
}

// ---- the drop-down -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// §64's overlay shape: a box over whatever is beneath, composed a row at a time
// and blitted (§68), with `drawn = false` on the way out as the whole of putting
// the screen back.
//
// It hangs from row one, so it covers the tab bar rather than starting below it -
// which is what makes it read as dropping out of the title above it.
const menuBoxY = 1

// Measured rather than assumed. The items are padded to a common width so the
// accelerators line up, and measuring anyway means a longer one added later
// widens the box instead of running out of it.
u16 menuBoxW() {
  u16 w
  u16 n

  w = 0

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < menuItemCount( menuAt() ); i++ ) {
    n = strLen( menuItemText( menuAt(), i ) )
    if ( n > w ) w = n
  }

  return w + 4
}

// Under its own title, pulled left only if it would run off the edge - which is
// `View` at forty columns, where the box is exactly as wide as what is left.
u16 menuBoxX() {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw

  bx = menuBarX( menuAt() ) - 1
  bw = menuBoxW()

  if ( bx + bw > cols ) bx = cols - bw

  return bx
}

// **The lit item is a hole the full width of the box**, not just under its text,
// so it reads as a bar rather than as a word that changed colour.
sub drawMenuItem( u16 i ) {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw
  u8  a

  bx = menuBoxX()
  bw = menuBoxW()

  a = attrStatus
  if ( i == menuItemAt() ) a = attrText

  rowPut( bx, boxV, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < bw - 1; x++ ) {
    rowPut( bx + x, ' ', a )
  }

  rowStr( bx + 2, menuItemText( menuAt(), i ), bx + bw - 1, a )
  rowPut( bx + bw - 1, boxV, attrStatus )

  rowBlit( menuBoxY + 1 + i, bx, bx + bw )
}

sub drawMenuEdge( u16 y, u8 left, u8 right ) {
  u16 bx
  u16 bw

  bx = menuBoxX()
  bw = menuBoxW()

  rowPut( bx, left, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < bw - 1; x++ ) {
    rowPut( bx + x, boxH, attrStatus )
  }

  rowPut( bx + bw - 1, right, attrStatus )
  rowBlit( y, bx, bx + bw )
}

sub drawMenuBox {
  u16 n
  n = menuItemCount( menuAt() )

  drawMenuEdge( menuBoxY, boxTL, boxTR )

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < n; i++ ) {
    drawMenuItem( i )
  }

  drawMenuEdge( menuBoxY + 1 + n, boxBL, boxBR )

  lastMenuAt   = menuAt()
  lastMenuItem = menuItemAt()
}

// The name with its directory taken off, **found by looking rather than by
// subtracting `dirPathLen`** - which is where the *panel* currently is, and the
// panel can be taken somewhere else with `Enter` after a file is open. Subtracting
// it would then chop the wrong number of characters off a name that never moved.
//
// 92 and 58 rather than escapes, the same pair `takeName` splits on: a drive
// letter ends a directory as surely as a backslash does.
u16 baseName( u16 at ) {
  u16 last
  u16 i

  last = 0
  i = 0

  while ( peek8( at + i ) != 0 ) {
    if ( peek8( at + i ) == 92 || peek8( at + i ) == 58 ) last = i + 1
    i++
  }

  return at + last
}

// **The live copy wins for the slot you are in.** §65 saves a name and a dirty
// flag into `docNames` and `docDirty` when you switch *away*, so for the document
// in front of you those arrays hold the state as of the last switch and
// `fileName` and `dirty` hold the truth. Every tab but one reads the arrays.
u16 tabNameAt( u16 d ) {
  if ( d == textDoc() ) {
    if ( !haveName ) return addr( sNewTab )
    return baseName( addr( fileName ) )
  }

  if ( docHave[d] == 0 ) return addr( sNewTab )
  return baseName( addr( docNames ) + d * nameMax )
}

bool tabDirty( u16 d ) => d == textDoc() ? dirty : docDirty[d] != 0

// One tab, returning where the next one starts. **The live one is a hole punched
// in the band** - normal video, continuous with the text pane under it - which is
// the explorer's trick fourteen columns away and the reason the obvious
// arrangement is inverted here. It also costs no attribute, which mode 7 could
// not have spared.
u16 drawTab( u16 x, u16 at, bool live, bool star ) {
  u8  a
  u16 n

  a = live ? attrText : attrStatus

  if ( x >= cols ) return x

  rowPut( x, ' ', a )
  x++

  n = 0
  while ( peek8( at + n ) != 0 && x < cols ) {
    rowPut( x, peek8( at + n ), a )
    x++
    n++
  }

  if ( star && x < cols ) {
    rowPut( x, '*', a )
    x++
  }

  if ( x < cols ) {
    rowPut( x, ' ', a )
    x++
  }

  return x
}

sub drawTabs {
  u16 x

  rowFill( 0, cols, ' ', attrStatus )

  x = 0

  // The panel is a tab here because it is a slot in §65's ring - which is the
  // visible half of a decision already made, rather than a new one. Hidden with
  // `^B` it leaves both the ring and the bar.
  if ( expShown ) x = drawTab( x, addr( sExpTab ), expFocus, false )

  for ( u16 d = 0; d < edMaxDocs; d++ ) {
    if ( docOpen[d] != 0 ) {
      x = drawTab( x, tabNameAt( d ), !expFocus && d == textDoc(), tabDirty( d ) )
    }
  }

  rowBlit( 1, 0, cols )
}

// The panel. Reverse video like the status line - which is the attribute that
// survives the mono translation unchanged, `0x70` meaning the same thing on an
// MDA as on a VGA - with the selected row in *normal*, so it reads as a hole
// punched in the panel rather than needing a colour mono does not have.
//
// The selection only shows while the panel has focus. Where the keys are going
// is the one thing a two-pane screen has to be unambiguous about, and a
// highlight that stays put when focus leaves is exactly how that gets lost.
sub drawExplorer {
  u16 h
  u16 i
  u16 at
  u16 n
  u16 thumbTop
  u16 thumbEnd
  u8  attr
  u8  ch

  h = edHeight()

  thumbTop = listThumbAt()
  thumbEnd = thumbTop + listThumbSize()

  for ( u16 y = 0; y < h; y++ ) {
    i = listTop() + y

    attr = attrStatus
    if ( expFocus && i == listCursor() && i < listCount() ) attr = attrText

    // The file being edited, marked rather than coloured - a third attribute is
    // not available in mono, and a column that is blank for every other row
    // costs nothing it was using.
    ch = 32
    if ( i < listCount() && haveName &&
         listIsNamed( i, addr( fileName ) + dirPathLen ) ) ch = 42

    cellAt( 0, edTop() + y, ch, attr )

    // The length is measured before the row is drawn, because `listLoad` writes
    // a name and its terminator and leaves the rest of the slot alone - reading
    // past the terminator would put the previous occupant of that slot on
    // screen.
    n = 0
    at = 0

    if ( i < listCount() ) {
      at = listName( i )
      while ( n < expName && peek8( at + n ) != 0 ) {
        n++
      }
    }

    for ( u16 x = 0; x < expName; x++ ) {
      cellAt( 1 + x, edTop() + y, x < n ? peek8( at + x ) : 32, attr )
    }

    // The track is always drawn in the panel attribute, so the hole does not
    // eat the scrollbar on the row the cursor happens to be on.
    ch = 0xB0
    if ( y >= thumbTop && y < thumbEnd ) ch = 0xDB

    cellAt( expCols - 1, edTop() + y, ch, attrStatus )
  }
}

// Clipped at `stop` rather than at the screen, because the status line has more
// than one thing on it and they are not all at the left. **The message and the
// line:column used to overlap**: the message was placed 26 columns from the
// right and is 30 long, so it ran under the numbers, which were drawn after it
// and won - leaving "file does not 1it :1 not op", a refusal made unreadable by
// the one thing on screen that never changes.
sub statusClip( u16 x, u16 at, u16 stop ) {
  u16 i
  i = 0

  while ( peek8( at + i ) != '$' && x + i < stop ) {
    rowPut( x + i, peek8( at + i ), attrStatus )
    i++
  }
}

sub statusText( u16 x, u16 at ) => statusClip( x, at, cols )

// The directory the panel is showing. Empty is where the editor was started,
// which DOS spells as nothing and a person reads as `.`.
u16 statusPath( u16 x ) {
  u16 i

  if ( dirPathLen == 0 ) {
    statusText( x, addr( sHere ) )
    return 1
  }

  i = 0
  while ( i < dirPathLen && x + i < cols ) {
    rowPut( x + i, dirPath[i], attrStatus )
    i++
  }

  return i
}

// **The name is ASCIZ and everything else here is `$`-terminated.** `statusText`
// was being handed it anyway and got away with it: the row is filled with spaces
// first and the zero bytes after the name render as blanks, so eighty cells of
// nothing looked exactly like a name. Returns the length, because the caller
// needs to know where the name ended and counting it separately is how two
// answers come to differ.
u16 statusName( u16 x ) {
  u16 i
  i = 0

  while ( fileName[i] != 0 && x + i < cols ) {
    rowPut( x + i, fileName[i], attrStatus )
    i++
  }

  return i
}

// The digits used to be worked out here, in the fourth copy of the same loop in
// the repository. `std/str.momo` has it now and this places what it wrote.
u8[numMaxDigits] numBuf

sub statusNumber( u16 x, u16 n ) {
  u16 used

  used = numText( addr( numBuf ), n )

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < used; i++ ) {
    rowPut( x + i, numBuf[i], attrStatus )
  }
}

// §60's buffer straight onto the status line. It is read through `fieldAddr`
// rather than copied, which is the same reason `textSave` hands DOS each chunk
// where it lies: nothing here needs a second copy of anything.
sub statusField( u16 x ) {
  u16 at
  at = fieldAddr()

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < fieldLength() && x + i < cols; i++ ) {
    rowPut( x + i, peek8( at + i ), attrStatus )
  }
}

// **A load from inside has nowhere to put dots.** At startup there is a DOS
// prompt underneath and `loud` prints one per read; from inside there is a
// document on screen and the status line is the only row that may be written
// over. `edit.com` uses the same row for the same reason.
//
// Kilobytes read rather than lines loaded, because the line count lives in the
// buffer and §58 is explicit that nothing may read the buffer inside `textBulk`.
// The loader knows how many bytes it has asked for, which is a fact about the
// file rather than about the buffer, so it is the one that can be shown.
sub showLoading( u16 kb ) {
  u16 x

  rowFill( 0, cols, ' ', attrStatus )

  statusText( 0, addr( sLoading ) )

  // Measured, not written down - the same rule the prompt label follows.
  x = strLen( addr( sLoading ) )

  x = x + statusName( x )

  statusNumber( x + 1, kb )
  statusText( x + 1 + numWidth( kb ), addr( sKb ) )

  rowBlit( rows - 1, 0, cols )
}

// **Composed, then written, and they are two routines** because the arm below
// returns early - a blit at every exit is a blit somebody will forget to add to
// the third one. §68 is the rule this shape exists to make unbreakable.
sub drawStatus {
  composeStatus()
  rowBlit( rows - 1, 0, cols )
}

sub composeStatus {
  rowFill( 0, cols, ' ', attrStatus )

  if ( prompting() ) {
    statusText( 0, askLabel )
    statusField( askAt )

    if ( message != 0 ) {
      statusText( askAt + fieldLength() + 2, message )

      // The one message with a number in it, and the number goes after the
      // words rather than into them. **The offset is measured rather than
      // written down**, which is the same rule the prompt label follows two
      // routines up: a 9 here agrees with `sDone` until somebody edits the
      // string, and then it is a column out in the one place a person is
      // reading a number.
      if ( message == addr( sDone ) ) {
        statusNumber( askAt + fieldLength() + 2 + strLen( message ), askCount )
      }
    }

    return
  }

  // A document with nowhere to go yet says so, rather than starting the line
  // with a name-shaped blank.
  // **While the panel has focus the line says where you are, not what you were
  // editing.** Twelve columns cannot hold a path, so the one row that can is
  // the one that says which directory those names are from - which is the
  // question descending into a subdirectory immediately raises.
  // **A message takes the whole line**, which §66's tab bar is what makes safe:
  // the name is up there now, so the only thing displaced is a path.
  //
  // It used to be squeezed in after the name and **skipped entirely below 41
  // columns**, on the grounds that there was no room - which is a refusal nobody
  // sees, and `^Q` is precisely the refusal that must not be missed.
  if ( message != 0 ) {
    statusClip( 0, message, cols - 13 )
  } else if ( expFocus ) {
    statusPath( 0 )
  } else if ( haveName ) {
    statusName( 0 )
  } else {
    statusText( 0, addr( sNoName ) )
  }

  statusNumber( cols - 12, viewLine() + 1 )
  rowPut( cols - 8, ':', attrStatus )
  statusNumber( cols - 7, viewCol() + 1 )
}

// The hardware cursor follows the logical one, so the caret is where the next
// character lands rather than wherever the last BIOS write left it. While the
// prompt is open the next character lands in the prompt, so that is where it
// goes - a caret left in the document would be pointing at the wrong thing.
sub placeCursor {
  // **There is one hardware cursor**, which §55 already noted for multiple
  // cursors and which a second pane runs into first. It goes where the keys are
  // going: the prompt while one is open, the panel while it has focus, the
  // document otherwise. Anything else and the screen is telling you the wrong
  // thing about where you are.
  if ( dlgUp() ) {
    moveTo( u8( dlgButtonX( dlgPick ) ), u8( dlgY() + 4 ) )
    return
  }

  if ( menuUp() ) {
    moveTo( u8( menuBoxX() + 2 ), u8( menuBoxY + 1 + menuItemAt() ) )
    return
  }

  if ( picking ) {
    moveTo( u8( pickX + pickGridX + ( pickCode & 15 ) * 2 ),
            u8( pickY + pickGridY + ( pickCode >> 4 ) ) )
    return
  }

  if ( expFocus && !prompting() ) {
    moveTo( 1, u8( edTop() + listCursor() - listTop() ) )
    return
  }

  if ( prompting() ) {
    moveTo( u8( askAt + fieldCursor() ), u8( rows - 1 ) )
    return
  }

  moveTo( u8( edLeft() + viewCol() - viewLeft() ),
          u8( edTop() + viewLine() - viewTop() ) )
}

// ---- how much of the screen an action actually owes -------------------------
//
// §55 shipped "the redraw is the whole screen, every keystroke" with the claim
// that 2,000 cells is comfortably inside the gap between two keys. Holding Down
// on a 286 overflowed the BIOS keyboard buffer after about ten lines and the
// machine beeped; `edit.com` on the same machine, subject to the same key
// repeat, managed most of a screen. `drawrate` is the measurement that was owed.
//
// Two things were being paid for and neither had to be:
//
//   a motion inside the window changes no cell of the text area at all, and was
//   redrawing every one of them
//
//   a one-line scroll changes one row, and §1 has no string instruction - but
//   the BIOS does, and `scrollUp` is one interrupt where this was 1,920 far
//   stores
//
// `needText` is set by anything that is not a pure motion, which is one line in
// `apply` rather than a flag at every edit site - conservative on purpose, since
// the cost of being wrong the safe way is one redraw and the cost of being wrong
// the other way is a stale screen.
bool needText

// What the last paint left on screen. A selection or an open prompt paints into
// the rows themselves, so both ends of either have to force the full path: the
// frame that clears a highlight has as much to do as the one that drew it.
u16  lastTop
u16  lastLeft
bool lastSel
bool lastAsk
bool lastPick
bool lastMenu
u16  lastMenuAt
u16  lastMenuItem
bool lastDlg
bool drawn

// Rebuilt from nothing, because the directory is not ours and anything may have
// written to it since - §62 makes that the rule rather than an option.
sub refreshList {
  listLoad( addr( dirPat ), dirDirectory )
  listSize( edHeight() )

  if ( listOverflowed() ) message = addr( sTooMany )

  pointAtCurrent()
}

// The cursor lands on the file being edited, which is the difference between a
// panel and a file picker: it says where you are, not only what is here.
//
// Split out of the rebuild because **switching documents wants this half and not
// the other**: the directory has not changed, only which of its files is in front
// of you, and re-reading a floppy to answer that would be felt.
sub pointAtCurrent {
  if ( !haveName ) return

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < listCount(); i++ ) {
    if ( listIsNamed( i, addr( fileName ) + dirPathLen ) ) {
      listGoto( i )
      return
    }
  }
}

// `^B`, which is VS Code's and was free. Showing it takes fourteen columns off
// the editor, so the window is resized and **the horizontal scroll re-clamped**:
// `viewSize` takes a width and has no opinion about a left edge that is now past
// it, so a document scrolled right would be looking at a column that no longer
// exists.
//
// `viewGoto` rather than a resize-and-follow, which would be a library routine
// with one caller. It sets the goal column as a side effect - so a Down after a
// toggle aims at the column the cursor is in rather than one it was remembering,
// which is the right answer after a layout change anyway.
sub toggleExplorer {
  expShown = !expShown
  expFocus = expShown

  if ( expShown ) refreshList()

  viewSize( edHeight(), edWidth() )
  viewGoto( viewLine(), viewCol() )

  needText = true
  needExp  = true
}

// `..` is the only entry that moves rather than opens.
bool isUp( u16 at ) => peek8( at ) == '.' && peek8( at + 1 ) == '.' &&
                      peek8( at + 2 ) == 0

// Another `..\` on the end. **DOS resolves the path and this does not have to**
// - `..\..\` is a directory like any other, so climbing needs no idea of how
// deep it started.
sub addUp {
  if ( dirPathLen + 3 >= len( dirPath ) ) return

  dirPath[dirPathLen]     = '.'
  dirPath[dirPathLen + 1] = '.'
  dirPath[dirPathLen + 2] = 92

  dirPathLen = dirPathLen + 3
}

sub goUp {
  u16 n

  // Already climbing, or standing where it started: another `..` either way.
  if ( dirPathLen == 0 ) {
    addUp()
    return
  }

  if ( dirPathLen >= 3 && dirPath[dirPathLen - 1] == 92 &&
       dirPath[dirPathLen - 2] == '.' && dirPath[dirPathLen - 3] == '.' ) {
    addUp()
    return
  }

  // Otherwise drop the last component, separator and all. A drive letter counts
  // as a separator, so `d:sub\` climbs to `d:` and not to nothing.
  n = dirPathLen - 1
  while ( n > 0 && dirPath[n - 1] != 92 && dirPath[n - 1] != 58 ) {
    n--
  }

  dirPathLen = n
}

sub goDown( u16 nameAt ) {
  u16 n

  n = 0
  while ( peek8( nameAt + n ) != 0 ) {
    n++
  }

  if ( dirPathLen + n + 1 >= len( dirPath ) ) return

  memCopy( addr( dirPath ) + dirPathLen, nameAt, n )
  dirPath[dirPathLen + n] = 92

  dirPathLen = dirPathLen + n + 1
}

sub descend( u16 nameAt ) {
  if ( isUp( nameAt ) ) {
    goUp()
  } else {
    goDown( nameAt )
  }

  buildPattern()
  refreshList()
  needExp = true
}

sub openSelected {
  u16 n

  if ( listCursor() >= listCount() ) return

  // Enter means "go there" on a directory and "edit this" on a file, which is
  // one key doing the obvious thing to whatever it is pointed at.
  if ( listIsDir( listCursor() ) ) {
    descend( listName( listCursor() ) )
    return
  }

  n = buildOpenPath( listName( listCursor() ) )

  // Focus follows the file, the way it does in VS Code - Enter in the panel means
  // "edit this", and leaving the caret in the list would make the next keystroke
  // go somewhere nobody was looking.
  if ( openNamed( addr( openPath ), n ) ) expFocus = false
}

// **The panel takes everything while it has focus**, the way the prompt does, so
// there is one place that answers "where did that keystroke go". `^Q` and `^B`
// are the two that cross the boundary, because leaving the program and putting
// the panel away should not need a detour through the editor first.
// Showing it if it is not, because a person reaching for the panel wants the
// panel rather than a lesson about which key reveals it.
sub focusExplorer {
  if ( !expShown ) {
    toggleExplorer()
    return
  }

  expFocus = true
  needExp  = true
}

// ---- switching between what is open -----------------------------------------

sub docSaveHere( u16 d ) {
  memCopy( addr( docNames ) + d * nameMax, addr( fileName ), nameMax )

  docHave[d]  = haveName ? 1 : 0
  docDirty[d] = dirty ? 1 : 0
}

sub docLoadHere( u16 d ) {
  memCopy( addr( fileName ), addr( docNames ) + d * nameMax, nameMax )

  haveName = docHave[d] != 0
  dirty    = docDirty[d] != 0
}

bool slotLive( u16 s ) => s == expSlot ? expShown : docOpen[s] != 0

u16 openDocs() {
  u16 n
  n = 0

  for ( u16 d = 0; d < edMaxDocs; d++ ) {
    if ( docOpen[d] != 0 ) n++
  }

  return n
}

u16 freeSlot() {
  for ( u16 d = 0; d < edMaxDocs; d++ ) {
    if ( docOpen[d] == 0 ) return d
  }

  return edMaxDocs
}

// **One call switches both**, because §56 switches §54 from inside `viewSwitch`.
// Two switches a caller has to remember to make together are two that will one
// day be made apart, and the symptom of that is one document's cursor over
// another document's text - which reads as a corrupt buffer rather than a missed
// call.
sub goToSlot( u16 slot ) {
  if ( slot == expSlot ) {
    focusExplorer()
    return
  }

  expFocus = false

  if ( slot != textDoc() ) {
    docSaveHere( textDoc() )
    viewSwitch( slot )
    docLoadHere( slot )

    if ( expShown ) pointAtCurrent()
  }

  needText = true
  needExp  = true
}

// Forward or back over the live slots, wrapping. A dead slot is skipped rather
// than shown empty, which is the whole of "an unused tab is invisible": there is
// nothing to look at and nothing to land on.
sub cycleDoc( bool forward ) {
  u16 at
  u16 by

  at = expFocus ? expSlot : textDoc()
  by = forward ? 1 : cycleSize - 1

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < cycleSize; i++ ) {
    at = ( at + by ) % cycleSize
    if ( slotLive( at ) ) break
  }

  goToSlot( at )
}

// `^T`. An empty, unnamed document in the first free slot - which is what a new
// tab is everywhere, and leaves `^O` and the panel as the two ways to put
// something in it.
sub newDoc {
  u16 d

  d = freeSlot()

  if ( d >= edMaxDocs ) {
    message = addr( sNoSlots )
    return
  }

  docSaveHere( textDoc() )
  viewSwitch( d )

  docOpen[d] = 1

  textClear()
  viewHome()

  fileName[0] = 0
  haveName = false
  dirty    = false

  expFocus = false
  needText = true
  needExp  = true
}

// `^W`. **It refuses rather than asking**, which is `^O`'s rule at the other end
// of the same problem: one keystroke, nothing said, and the only copy gone. A
// prompt here would be a second kind of answer to a question this program has
// already decided how to answer once.
//
// The last document does not close, because an editor with no document in it is
// a state with nothing to draw and no way out except quitting.
sub closeDoc {
  u16 d

  if ( openDocs() < 2 ) {
    message = addr( sOneDoc )
    return
  }

  if ( dirty ) {
    message = addr( sDirty )
    return
  }

  d = textDoc()

  // The chunks go back to the shared store before the slot is marked free, since
  // a slot nobody is in is a slot nobody will clear.
  textClear()
  docOpen[d] = 0

  // A document rather than whatever is next in the ring: closing a file and
  // landing in the explorer is a pane change nobody asked for. There is always
  // one, because two were open a moment ago.
  for ( u16 i = 0; i < edMaxDocs; i++ ) {
    d = ( d + 1 ) % edMaxDocs
    if ( docOpen[d] != 0 ) break
  }

  goToSlot( d )
}

sub explorerKey( u16 k ) {
  needExp = true

  // **`Ctrl+Tab` leaves, the same way it arrived.** Tab used to do this and has
  // been given back to the document, where a person indenting a line wants it -
  // which was the whole argument for moving to browser-style cycling.
  //
  // `selecting` is what is left of Shift after §57's fold, so `Ctrl+Shift+Tab`
  // needs no row of its own: the two differ in one flag bit and nothing else.
  if ( k == keyExt + keyCtrlTab ) {
    cycleDoc( !selecting )
    return
  }

  if ( k == keyQuit ) {
    tryQuit()
    return
  }

  if ( k == 2 ) {
    toggleExplorer()
    return
  }

  // `^T` opens a tab from in here too, because "a new file in this directory" is
  // a thought somebody has while looking at the directory. `^W` is deliberately
  // not here: closing from a pane that is not a document leaves the question of
  // which document, and the answer would have to be invented.
  if ( k == 20 ) {
    newDoc()
    return
  }

  // Escape leaves the panel on screen and gives the keys back. `^B` is what puts
  // it away - two gestures because they are two intentions, and a person who
  // wanted the columns back will reach for the one that took them.
  if ( k == keyEsc ) {
    expFocus = false
    return
  }

  if ( k == 13 ) {
    openSelected()
    return
  }

  // **A printable key jumps to the next entry starting with it**, which is what
  // every file list has done since before this machine. It is last, so the keys
  // above keep their meanings - and it takes the whole printable range rather
  // than letters alone, because a directory full of `8086*.ASM` is exactly the
  // case somebody would try it on.
  if ( k >= 32 && k < 127 ) {
    listJump( u8( k ) )
    return
  }

  if ( k == keyExt + keyUp )    listUp()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyDown )  listDown()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyPgUp )  listPageUp()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyPgDn )  listPageDown()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyHome )  listHome()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyEnd )   listEnd()
}

// ---- the character picker (§64) ---------------------------------------------
//
// Sixteen by sixteen, because that is how a codepage is drawn: the row is the
// high nibble and the column is the low one, so the cursor's position **is** the
// character's code and the axes are how you read it off. The alternative shapes
// were four rows of 64 and eight of 32, and 40-column mode answered both - 64
// does not fit at all, and 32 fits only with the glyphs jammed edge to edge,
// which is unreadable exactly where it matters, since box-drawing characters
// butted together form continuous lines with no seam to see.
//
// **The whole grid is on screen at once**, which is what the shape buys: there
// is nothing to scroll and nothing to page, so the keys are the arrows, the ends
// of a row, the ends of the set, and Enter. That is the entire interface.
//
// What it is *for* is the range no keyboard sends: 0-31 and 127-255 are real
// glyphs in codepage 437 - smileys, arrows, box drawing, the accented letters -
// and cells are written straight to the frame, so all 256 draw. `Alt`+decimal
// already reaches the same characters for somebody who knows the number, which
// is why the information line says what the number is: this is the way in, and
// it teaches the faster way out.
const pickGridX = 4
const pickGridY = 2

// Derived rather than picked, the same rule the prompt label follows. A border, a
// space, a row label, a space, then sixteen glyphs one apart, a space and a
// border: 37 columns, which is three inside a 40-column screen. Down the side:
// a border, the column header, sixteen rows, a rule, the information line and a
// border - 21 rows, four inside 25.
const pickW = pickGridX + 16 * 2 - 1 + 2
const pickH = pickGridY + 16 + 3

// Codepage 437's box, which the MDA font has as well as the VGA's - so the frame
// draws in `mode mono` for the same reason the rest of this does.
const boxH  = 196
const boxV  = 179
const boxTL = 218
const boxTR = 191
const boxBL = 192
const boxBR = 217
const boxML = 195
const boxMR = 180

bool picking
u16  pickCode
u16  pickX
u16  pickY

// **Ten and thirteen are refused, and the reason is §54's rather than a taste.**
// `textLoad` drops a carriage return and starts a line at a newline, so those two
// bytes are what a text file's lines are *made of*: inserted, saved and read back
// they would not be the characters that went in. Every other byte survives the
// round trip, zero included, because nothing in §54 is terminated - a line is a
// length and a chain of chunks.
//
// They are refused where the cursor lands rather than where Enter is pressed, so
// the answer is on screen before the question is asked.
bool pickEnds( u16 c ) => c == 10 || c == 13

u8 hexDigit( u16 n ) {
  if ( n < 10 ) return u8( n + '0' )
  return u8( n - 10 + 'A' )
}

// Everything below composes into §68's row buffer in box coordinates and one
// routine blits the span. The box used to fill each row with spaces and then
// write over it, which is the flicker somebody watched while arrowing around the
// grid.
sub pickPut( u16 x, u8 ch, u8 attr ) {
  rowPut( pickX + x, ch, attr )
}

sub pickBlitRow( u16 y ) {
  rowBlit( pickY + y, pickX, pickX + pickW )
}

sub pickText( u16 x, u16 at ) {
  rowStr( pickX + x, at, pickX + pickW, attrStatus )
}

sub pickNumber( u16 x, u16 n ) {
  u16 used

  used = numText( addr( numBuf ), n )

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < used; i++ ) {
    pickPut( x + i, numBuf[i], attrStatus )
  }
}

sub pickRuleInto( u8 left, u8 right ) {
  pickPut( 0, left, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < pickW - 1; x++ ) {
    pickPut( x, boxH, attrStatus )
  }

  pickPut( pickW - 1, right, attrStatus )
}

sub pickBlankInto {
  pickPut( 0, boxV, attrStatus )

  for ( u16 x = 1; x < pickW - 1; x++ ) {
    pickPut( x, ' ', attrStatus )
  }

  pickPut( pickW - 1, boxV, attrStatus )
}

// Reverse video for the box and *normal* for the cell under the cursor, which is
// the explorer's trick and is here for the explorer's reason: it reads as a hole
// punched in the panel, and 0x70 against 0x07 means the same thing on an MDA as
// on a VGA. A colour would not.
sub pickGridRow( u16 r ) {
  u16 code
  u8  attr

  pickBlankInto()
  pickPut( 2, hexDigit( r ), attrStatus )

  for ( u16 c = 0; c < 16; c++ ) {
    code = r * 16 + c

    attr = attrStatus
    if ( code == pickCode ) attr = attrText

    pickPut( pickGridX + c * 2, u8( code ), attr )
  }

  pickBlitRow( pickGridY + r )
}

// **One cell of the grid, straight to the frame.** Two of these and the
// information line are the whole of what moving the cursor changes, against the
// 37x21 the box was redrawing per keystroke. Written once with its final value,
// so it is not buffered: the buffer is for rows that would otherwise be cleared
// first, and this one never is.
sub pickGlyphAt( u16 code ) {
  u8 attr

  attr = attrStatus
  if ( code == pickCode ) attr = attrText

  cellAt( pickX + pickGridX + ( code & 15 ) * 2,
          pickY + pickGridY + ( code >> 4 ), u8( code ), attr )
}

// Decimal, hex and the `Alt` sequence. The decimal is in the hint already and is
// labelled separately anyway, because the two are read for different things: one
// is what to press next time and the other is what to look up in a table.
sub pickInfo {
  composePickInfo()
  pickBlitRow( pickH - 2 )
}

// Split from the blit for `drawStatus`'s reason: the refusal arm returns early,
// and a write at each exit is one somebody will forget to add to the third.
sub composePickInfo {
  u16 x

  pickBlankInto()

  x = 2

  pickText( x, addr( sPickDec ) )
  x = x + strLen( addr( sPickDec ) )

  pickNumber( x, pickCode )
  x = x + numWidth( pickCode )

  pickText( x, addr( sPickHex ) )
  x = x + strLen( addr( sPickHex ) )

  pickPut( x, hexDigit( pickCode >> 4 ), attrStatus )
  pickPut( x + 1, hexDigit( pickCode & 15 ), attrStatus )
  x = x + 2

  if ( pickEnds( pickCode ) ) {
    pickText( x, addr( sPickEol ) )
    return
  }

  pickText( x, addr( sPickAlt ) )
  x = x + strLen( addr( sPickAlt ) )

  pickNumber( x, pickCode )
}

// A rule with something written along it - the title and the key hints both sit
// on one - which is two things on one row and so has to be composed before any of
// it is written, the same as everything else here.
sub pickRuleRow( u16 y, u8 left, u8 right, u16 label ) {
  pickRuleInto( left, right )
  pickText( 2, label )
  pickBlitRow( y )
}

sub drawPicker {
  pickRuleRow( 0, boxTL, boxTR, addr( sPickTitle ) )

  pickBlankInto()
  for ( u16 c = 0; c < 16; c++ ) {
    pickPut( pickGridX + c * 2, hexDigit( c ), attrStatus )
  }
  pickBlitRow( pickGridY - 1 )

  for ( u16 r = 0; r < 16; r++ ) {
    pickGridRow( r )
  }

  pickRuleInto( boxML, boxMR )
  pickBlitRow( pickH - 3 )

  pickInfo()

  pickRuleRow( pickH - 1, boxBL, boxBR, addr( sPickKeys ) )

  // What the cheap path below measures against.
  lastPickCode = pickCode
}

// **What a keystroke in the picker actually changes**: the cell losing the
// highlight, the cell gaining it, and the information line. The box was being
// redrawn whole for that, which is 777 cells for three.
u16 lastPickCode

sub pickMoved {
  if ( pickCode == lastPickCode ) return

  pickGlyphAt( lastPickCode )
  pickGlyphAt( pickCode )
  pickInfo()

  lastPickCode = pickCode
}

// **The box is never clipped**, it is refused. Every text mode this can be
// launched into is at least 40x25 and the box is 37x21, so the guard is against
// a mode nobody has rather than a screen somebody has - and the alternative is a
// bounds test on each of four hundred cells to protect against nothing.
sub openPicker {
  message = 0

  if ( cols < pickW || rows < pickH ) {
    message = addr( sPickSmall )
    return
  }

  picking = true
  pickX = ( cols - pickW ) / 2
  pickY = ( rows - pickH ) / 2
}

// `drawn` going false is the whole of putting the screen back: the box covered
// the text, the panel and nothing else, and both of those redraw on `!drawn`.
sub closePicker {
  picking = false
  drawn = false
}

sub pickTake {
  if ( pickEnds( pickCode ) ) return

  closePicker()

  // The same character either way, and **the two insert paths are different
  // questions**. `doInsert` takes a byte already; `fieldKey` takes a *keystroke*
  // and has a range that stops short of the control codes on purpose, which is
  // exactly the range this exists to reach - so §60 grew `fieldPut` for the
  // caller that has already decided the answer is a character.
  if ( prompting() ) {
    fieldPut( u8( pickCode ) )
  } else {
    doInsert( u8( pickCode ) )
  }
}

// The grid is a strip of 256 codes shown sixteen wide, so left and right are one
// less and one more and up and down are sixteen - each masked back into the byte,
// which makes the wrap at either end free and leaves no edge case to get wrong.
sub pickerKey( u16 k ) {
  // Shift means nothing in here, so it is folded out rather than ignored: a
  // shifted arrow is still an arrow, and a hand coming off a selection should
  // not find the picker inert.
  if ( k >= keyShift ) k = keyExt + ( k - keyShift )

  if ( k == keyEsc || k == 16 ) {
    closePicker()
    return
  }

  if ( k == 13 ) {
    pickTake()
    return
  }

  if ( k == keyExt + keyLeft )     pickCode = ( pickCode + 255 ) & 255
  if ( k == keyExt + keyRight )    pickCode = ( pickCode + 1 ) & 255
  if ( k == keyExt + keyUp )       pickCode = ( pickCode + 240 ) & 255
  if ( k == keyExt + keyDown )     pickCode = ( pickCode + 16 ) & 255
  if ( k == keyExt + keyHome )     pickCode = pickCode & 0xF0
  if ( k == keyExt + keyEnd )      pickCode = pickCode | 0x0F
  if ( k == keyExt + keyCtrlHome ) pickCode = 0
  if ( k == keyExt + keyCtrlEnd )  pickCode = 255
}

sub repaint {
  u16 h
  bool sel

  // **The box covers what it is over**, so a frame that already had it up has
  // nothing behind it worth drawing - which matters because the picker can be
  // opened from a prompt, and an open prompt is one of the conditions below that
  // forces a whole-screen redraw. Without this, arrowing around the grid would
  // cost 1,584 cells a keystroke to paint something the box is standing on.
  if ( picking && lastPick ) {
    pickMoved()
    drawStatus()
    placeCursor()
    return
  }

  // One row changes when the focus moves between buttons, and the box is over
  // everything - so this is the picker's arrangement with a smaller moving part.
  if ( dlgUp() && lastDlg ) {
    dlgButtons()
    drawStatus()
    placeCursor()
    return
  }

  // The same shape for the drop-down, and only while it is the *same* menu:
  // moving to another one has to erase the old box, which is what the full path
  // below does by drawing everything and then the new box over it.
  if ( menuUp() && lastMenu && menuAt() == lastMenuAt ) {
    if ( menuItemAt() != lastMenuItem ) {
      drawMenuItem( lastMenuItem )
      drawMenuItem( menuItemAt() )
      lastMenuItem = menuItemAt()
    }

    drawStatus()
    placeCursor()
    return
  }

  h = edHeight()
  sel = viewSelected()

  if ( !drawn || needText || sel || lastSel || prompting() || lastAsk ||
       viewLeft() != lastLeft ) {
    viewRender()
  } else if ( viewTop() == lastTop + 1 ) {
    scrollUp( u8( edLeft() ), u8( edTop() ), u8( edWidth() ), u8( h ), 1, attrText )
    viewRenderRow( h - 1 )
  } else if ( lastTop == viewTop() + 1 ) {
    scrollDown( u8( edLeft() ), u8( edTop() ), u8( edWidth() ), u8( h ), 1, attrText )
    viewRenderRow( 0 )
  } else if ( viewTop() != lastTop ) {
    // A page, a goto, or a find. More than one row is new and nothing here can
    // say which, so this is the case the whole-screen redraw is right for.
    viewRender()
  }

  // The panel is 336 cells at 80x25 against the text area's 1,584, so it gets
  // the same treatment: drawn when something it shows has changed, and not on a
  // keystroke that only moved the caret about the document.
  if ( expShown && ( needExp || !drawn ) ) drawExplorer()

  // **On `needExp`, which is already the flag for "something structural moved".**
  // A pair of rows redrawn every frame would be 160 cells against the 160 the
  // scroll path spends in total, and `drawrate` is why that is not free. Reusing
  // the panel's flag rather than adding a fifth follows `needText`'s rule: one
  // conservative trigger beats a flag at every site that might need it.
  if ( needExp || !drawn ) {
    drawMenus()
    drawTabs()
  }

  if ( menuUp() ) drawMenuBox()
  if ( picking ) drawPicker()

  // **Last, because it is the one that can be over another.** Save As opens it
  // with a prompt behind, and Exit with a menu behind.
  if ( dlgUp() ) drawDialog()

  drawStatus()
  placeCursor()

  needExp = false
  needText = false
  lastTop  = viewTop()
  lastLeft = viewLeft()
  lastSel  = sel
  lastAsk  = prompting()
  lastPick = picking
  lastMenu = menuUp()
  lastDlg  = dlgUp()
  drawn    = true
}

// ---- what a key does ----

const actNone   = 0
const actInsert = 1
const actLeft   = 2
const actRight  = 3
const actUp     = 4
const actDown   = 5
const actHome   = 6
const actEnd    = 7
const actBack   = 8
const actDelete = 9
const actEnter  = 10
const actUndo   = 11
const actSave   = 12
const actTop    = 13
const actBottom = 14
const actPgUp   = 15
const actPgDn   = 16
const actRedo   = 17
const actFind   = 18
const actAgain  = 19
const actCopy   = 20
const actCut    = 21
const actPaste  = 22
const actAll    = 23
const actWordL  = 24
const actWordR  = 25
const actGoto   = 26
const actOpen   = 27
const actRepl   = 28
const actExplor = 29
const actCycle  = 30
const actIndent = 31
const actNewDoc = 32
const actShut   = 33

// **Reachable from a menu and from nowhere else yet.** `Save As` was a branch
// inside `^S`, `Exit` was `^Q` handled beside the table, and the picker was `^P`
// handled ahead of the prompt - so a menu that could only run bound actions
// would have had three holes in it.
const actSaveAs = 34
const actQuit   = 35
const actPick   = 36

// ---- the menus (§67) ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// The titles are one array of arrays (§53) so that the bar's layout is **derived
// rather than written twice**: what used to sit here was a label string and a
// list of the columns its words started at, which is the same fact in two forms.
//
// Each item is padded to a common width so the accelerator lines up. The box is
// still measured rather than assuming that, because a menu somebody edits later
// should widen rather than overflow.
const u8[][] menuNames = [ "File$", "Edit$", "Search$", "View$" ]

// A menu is menu, item, character - three levels, and §53 has two. So each menu's
// labels are an array of arrays of their own and `menuItems` is §51's table over
// them, which is the one place this reads a spine by hand: two bytes an item.
const u8[][] mFile   = [ "New          ^T$",
                         "Open...      ^O$",
                         "Save         ^S$",
                         "Save As...     $",
                         "Close        ^W$",
                         "Exit         ^Q$" ]

const u8[][] mEdit   = [ "Undo         ^Z$",
                         "Redo         ^Y$",
                         "Cut          ^X$",
                         "Copy         ^C$",
                         "Paste        ^V$",
                         "Select All   ^A$",
                         "Character... ^P$" ]

const u8[][] mSearch = [ "Find...      ^F$",
                         "Find Next    ^L$",
                         "Replace...   ^R$",
                         "Go to Line   ^G$" ]

const u8[][] mView   = [ "Explorer     ^B$" ]

const u16[] menuItems = [ addr( mFile ), addr( mEdit ), addr( mSearch ), addr( mView ) ]

// **The action array is the authority for how many items a menu has**, and its
// length is §53's length spine. A menu's labels and its actions are an index
// apart, which is the shape that cost a session in §55's binding tables - so
// `npm run drift` holds each menu's label count against its action count, because
// a label list shorter than its actions would read a word past its spine as an
// address and draw whatever that pointed at.
const u8[][] menuActs = [
  [ actNewDoc, actOpen, actSave, actSaveAs, actShut, actQuit ],
  [ actUndo, actRedo, actCut, actCopy, actPaste, actAll, actPick ],
  [ actFind, actAgain, actRepl, actGoto ],
  [ actExplor ]
]

// ---- what §67 asks the program (§37) ----

u16 menuCount() => len( menuNames )
u16 menuTitle( u16 m ) => addr( menuNames[m] )
u16 menuItemCount( u16 m ) => len( menuActs[m] )
u16 menuItemText( u16 m, u16 i ) => peek16( menuItems[m] + 2 * i )
u8 menuAction( u16 m, u16 i ) => menuActs[m][i]

// Where each title starts on the bar, from the titles themselves - two spaces
// between, one at the left. A menu renamed moves its own drop-down.
u16 menuBarX( u16 m ) {
  u16 x
  x = 1

  for ( u16 i = 0; i < m; i++ ) {
    x = x + strLen( menuTitle( i ) ) + 2
  }

  return x
}

const u8[] sExpTab = "Explorer\0"
const u8[] sNewTab = "(untitled)\0"

// No chord in this table yet, and the machinery is kept rather than removed:
// `^K ^C` for a comment toggle is the obvious first one, and `edloop` holds
// the mechanism against a script meanwhile. Every prefix here being 0 costs one
// comparison per entry per keystroke, which at this rate is nothing.
//
// **A row per binding** (§52), so a key, its prefix and its action cannot fall
// out of step: a row short of a column is the parser's error, and a row count
// that disagrees with `bindCount` is the resolver's. They were three parallel
// arrays until then, and drifted apart once - adding `^B` put an entry in two of
// them, so every row after it read the previous row's prefix and the *last* one
// read past the end entirely. `Ctrl+End` was that last row, and `Ctrl+Home`
// beside it worked perfectly, which is what made it look like a keyboard
// question rather than an off-by-one.
//
// What the form cannot catch is a key bound twice - the scan takes the first
// match and the second row never runs - so `npm run drift` still reads the key
// column for that.
const bindCount = 32

const group bind[bindCount] {
  u16 prefix
  u16 key
  u8  action
} = [
  [ 0, keyExt + keyLeft,      actLeft   ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyRight,     actRight  ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyUp,        actUp     ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyDown,      actDown   ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyHome,      actHome   ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyEnd,       actEnd    ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyDelete,    actDelete ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyPgUp,      actPgUp   ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyPgDn,      actPgDn   ],
  [ 0, 8,                     actBack   ], // Backspace
  [ 0, 13,                    actEnter  ], // Enter
  [ 0, 26,                    actUndo   ], // ^Z
  [ 0, 19,                    actSave   ], // ^S
  [ 0, 25,                    actRedo   ], // ^Y
  [ 0, 6,                     actFind   ], // ^F
  [ 0, 12,                    actAgain  ], // ^L
  [ 0, 3,                     actCopy   ], // ^C
  [ 0, 24,                    actCut    ], // ^X
  [ 0, 22,                    actPaste  ], // ^V
  [ 0, 1,                     actAll    ], // ^A
  [ 0, 7,                     actGoto   ], // ^G
  [ 0, 15,                    actOpen   ], // ^O
  [ 0, 18,                    actRepl   ], // ^R
  [ 0, 2,                     actExplor ], // ^B
  [ 0, keyExt + keyCtrlLeft,  actWordL  ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyCtrlRight, actWordR  ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyCtrlHome,  actTop    ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyCtrlEnd,   actBottom ],
  [ 0, keyExt + keyCtrlTab,   actCycle  ], // and Ctrl+Shift+Tab, which differs in one flag bit
  [ 0, 9,                     actIndent ], // Tab
  [ 0, 20,                    actNewDoc ], // ^T
  [ 0, 23,                    actShut   ]  // ^W
]

// ---- the menu bar, driven (§67) ----------------------------------------------
//
// **The scancodes are in the order the menus are.** `Alt`+letter reports the
// letter key's own scancode with nothing in the low byte, which §57 measured, and
// there is no arithmetic from a letter to its scancode - so this is a table, and
// the one thing it has to agree with is the order of `sMenuNames`.
//
// A disagreement is an `Alt` key that opens the wrong menu, which is visible on
// the first press. Out of range is refused by `menuShow` rather than read past
// the end of anything.
const u16[] menuAlt = [
  keyExt + keyAltF,
  keyExt + keyAltE,
  keyExt + keyAltS,
  keyExt + keyAltV
]

sub openMenu( u16 m ) {
  message = 0
  menuShow( m )
  needExp = true
}

// `F10` opens the first, which is what `edit.com` and Turbo Pascal do and is what
// somebody who has used a DOS editor will try - and §57 measured that `Alt` on
// its own is not a key, so there is no other way in without a letter.
bool menuOpened( u16 k ) {
  if ( k == keyExt + keyF10 ) {
    openMenu( 0 )
    return true
  }

  for ( u16 m = 0; m < len( menuAlt ); m++ ) {
    if ( k == menuAlt[m] ) {
      openMenu( m )
      return true
    }
  }

  return false
}

// **`needExp` on every keystroke**, which is §66's trigger for "something
// structural moved" and is what redraws the bar's highlight when the open menu
// changes. The cheap path in `repaint` does not consult it, so this costs
// nothing on the frames that take that path.
sub menuStep( u16 k ) {
  u8  r
  u16 was

  needExp = true
  was = menuAt()

  r = menuKey( k )

  // **A box that has moved is a close and an open**, and everything it was
  // standing on has to be put back - which `needExp` alone does not do. It
  // redraws the panel and the bars and leaves the text pane, so moving *right*
  // looked correct only because the panel happens to sit under the columns a
  // rightward move vacates, and moving *left* vacates columns nothing owned.
  //
  // A bug that is right in one direction is a bug about what is underneath
  // rather than about the thing on top.
  if ( menuAt() != was ) drawn = false

  if ( r == menuGoing ) return

  // Either way the box has to come off the screen, and `drawn` going false is the
  // whole of that - the same as closing the picker.
  drawn = false

  if ( r == menuChosen ) apply( menuAction( menuAt(), menuItemAt() ), 0 )
}

// Quitting is not in the table, because a loop has to be able to stop whatever
// the table says and a rebindable quit is a rebindable way to lose the door.
const keyQuit = 17

// Set while a Save As is on its way to quitting, so that answering **Yes** on an
// untitled document asks for a name, saves, and then goes - rather than asking
// for a name and leaving you where you started.
bool quitAfterSave

// **Any document, not the one in front of you.** With two open, the one holding
// unsaved work may not be the one on screen, which is exactly the case where a
// door that asked nothing would be worst.
bool anyDirty() {
  if ( dirty ) return true

  for ( u16 d = 0; d < edMaxDocs; d++ ) {
    if ( d != textDoc() && docOpen[d] != 0 && docDirty[d] != 0 ) return true
  }

  return false
}

// **It refuses, the way `^O` and `^W` do**, and the way through is to press it
// again. One rule for all three doors, and a second press is unambiguous where a
// dialog would be a seventh thing the prompt has to serve.
//
// `^Q` has never checked this, and with one file that was one file's work. §66
// put the door on the way to a menu, where **File > Exit** inherits it and it
// stops being a key somebody has to know about.
sub tryQuit {
  if ( !anyDirty() ) {
    running = false
    return
  }

  // **Switch to the document being asked about.** With two open, the one holding
  // unsaved work may not be the one on screen, and a box naming a file you cannot
  // see is a box asking you to take its word for it. It also makes the loop below
  // need no machinery: Yes saves this one and calls this again, which either
  // leaves or moves to the next.
  if ( !dirty ) {
    for ( u16 d = 0; d < edMaxDocs; d++ ) {
      if ( docOpen[d] != 0 && docDirty[d] != 0 ) {
        goToSlot( d )
        break
      }
    }
  }

  dlgOpen( dlgExit, addr( sSaveChanges ), tabNameAt( textDoc() ) )
}

// The name goes into `fileName` here and nowhere earlier, which is what makes
// Cancel cost nothing.
sub saveAsCommit {
  memCopy( addr( fileName ), addr( saveAsName ), nameMax )
  haveName = true

  doSave()

  // A file that did not exist a moment ago is in the directory now.
  if ( expShown ) refreshList()

  if ( quitAfterSave && !dirty ) tryQuit()
}

// Whether there is something there to lose. `fileCreate` truncates before a byte
// is written, so this has to be asked *before* the save rather than reported
// after it.
bool fileHere( u16 at ) {
  u16 h

  h = fileOpen( at, fileReadOnly )
  if ( fileFailed() ) return false

  fileClose( h )
  return true
}

// ---- what each answer does ----
//
// **The reason code is what makes one box serve two questions**, which is the
// prompt's shape one section over: `asking` says what Enter means there, and this
// says what Yes means here.
sub dlgAnswer( u16 pick ) {
  u8 why

  why = dlgWhy
  dlgWhy = dlgNone
  drawn = false

  if ( why == dlgExit ) {
    if ( pick == 0 ) {
      // Yes. `doSave` asks for a name when there is none, and then this is not
      // the end of it - `saveAsCommit` picks the thread back up.
      quitAfterSave = true
      doSave()

      if ( !dirty && !prompting() ) tryQuit()
      return
    }

    if ( pick == 1 ) running = false

    // Cancel, which is the answer a double-tap did not have.
    quitAfterSave = false
    return
  }

  if ( why == dlgOverwrite ) {
    if ( pick == 0 ) {
      saveAsCommit()
      return
    }

    // **No goes back to the question**, because "do not replace that one" almost
    // always means "let me pick another name". Cancel is what abandons the save,
    // and that distinction is why there are three buttons rather than two.
    if ( pick == 1 ) {
      promptOpen( askSave, addr( sSave ) )
      return
    }

    quitAfterSave = false
  }
}

sub dlgKey( u16 k ) {
  if ( k >= keyShift ) k = keyExt + ( k - keyShift )

  if ( k == keyEsc ) {
    dlgAnswer( dlgCount() - 1 )
    return
  }

  if ( k == 13 ) {
    dlgAnswer( dlgPick )
    return
  }

  if ( k == keyExt + keyLeft )  dlgPick = ( dlgPick + dlgCount() - 1 ) % dlgCount()
  if ( k == keyExt + keyRight ) dlgPick = ( dlgPick + 1 ) % dlgCount()
  if ( k == keyTab )            dlgPick = ( dlgPick + 1 ) % dlgCount()

  // **A letter chooses outright here**, which §67's menu items could not do: Y, N
  // and C are distinct, so there is nothing to cycle through and nothing to mark.
  if ( k >= 32 && k < keyExt ) {
    for ( u16 i = 0; i < dlgCount(); i++ ) {
      if ( fold( u8( k ) ) == fold( peek8( dlgButton( i ) ) ) ) {
        dlgAnswer( i )
        return
      }
    }
  }
}



u16 pending
bool running

u8 keyAction( u16 k ) {
  for ( u16 i = 0; i < len( bind ); i++ ) {
    if ( bind[i].key == k && bind[i].prefix == pending ) return bind[i].action
  }
  return actNone
}

bool keyIsPrefix( u16 k ) {
  for ( u16 i = 0; i < len( bind ); i++ ) {
    if ( bind[i].prefix == k ) return true
  }
  return false
}

sub insertOne( u8 ch ) {
  lineInsert( viewLine(), viewCol(), ch )
  viewGoto( viewLine(), viewCol() + 1 )
}

// **The group is opened only when there is a selection to kill.** Opening one
// breaks the run before it, so wrapping every keystroke would turn a run of
// typing into one action per character - §54's coalescing undone by a bracket
// that looked harmless. A command is a new action; a character is not.
sub doInsert( u8 ch ) {
  if ( viewSelected() ) {
    undoStep {
      killSelection()
      insertOne( ch )
    }
  } else {
    insertOne( ch )
  }

  dirty = true
}

// **Tab types spaces to the next stop**, which is what gives the key back
// without §56 learning about display columns. A literal tab is one byte and the
// arithmetic of a whole editor: a document column would stop being a screen
// column, and the cursor, the selection and the match highlight all measure in
// screen columns today. That is its own piece of work and is written up rather
// than half done - and note that a file *containing* tabs already renders one to
// a column, so the gap is there either way.
//
// Two, because that is what every source file in this repository is indented
// with and this editor's first job is those files. One constant.
const edTabWidth = 2

sub doIndent {
  u16 n

  n = edTabWidth - viewCol() % edTabWidth

  // A selection goes first, exactly as it does for a typed character. Indenting
  // the selected *block* is what a larger editor does here and is a different
  // command wearing the same key; this one is consistent with the rest of the
  // keyboard, and is one `^Z` away from being wrong about it.
  if ( viewSelected() ) {
    undoStep {
      killSelection()

      for ( u16 i = 0; i < n; i++ ) {
        insertOne( ' ' )
      }
    }
  } else {
    // No group, for `doInsert`'s reason: opening one breaks the run of typing
    // before it, and a Tab in the middle of typing is not a new action.
    for ( u16 i = 0; i < n; i++ ) {
      insertOne( ' ' )
    }
  }

  dirty = true
}

sub splitOne {
  lineSplit( viewLine(), viewCol() )
  viewGoto( viewLine() + 1, 0 )
}

sub doEnter {
  if ( viewSelected() ) {
    undoStep {
      killSelection()
      splitOne()
    }
  } else {
    splitOne()
  }

  dirty = true
}

// Backspace at column 0 is a join, which is why §54 records one as an undoable
// edit. The previous line's length has to be read before the join, because
// afterwards it is the length of both.
sub doBack {
  u16 ln
  u16 col
  u16 prevLen

  // A selection makes Backspace and forward Delete the same command, which is
  // what every editor does and is why neither needs a case of its own below.
  if ( killSelection() ) return

  ln = viewLine()
  col = viewCol()

  if ( col > 0 ) {
    lineDelete( ln, col - 1 )
    viewGoto( ln, col - 1 )
    dirty = true
    return
  }

  if ( ln == 0 ) return

  prevLen = lineLength( ln - 1 )
  lineJoin( ln - 1 )
  viewGoto( ln - 1, prevLen )
  dirty = true
}

// An undo that left the cursor where it was would put it somewhere the document
// no longer has, so §54 reports where the entry acted and the cursor follows.
sub doUndo {
  undoOnce()
  viewGoto( undoLine(), undoCol() )
  dirty = true
}

sub doSave {
  // **No name is a question, not a failure.** An editor started with no
  // arguments has a document and nowhere to put it, and the prompt already
  // serves five questions - this is the sixth and it needed no new mechanism.
  if ( !haveName ) {
    promptOpen( askSave, addr( sSave ) )
    return
  }

  if ( saveFile() ) {
    dirty = false
    return
  }

  // Through `message` rather than written onto the status line here, which is
  // what it used to do: the next redraw painted over it, so the one thing the
  // editor most needs to say was the one thing it said for a single frame.
  message = addr( sFailed )
}

// ---- the selection ----

// Whatever is selected, gone, with the cursor left where the selection started.
// **Everything that types over a selection goes through here** - a character, a
// Backspace, an Enter, a paste - so there is one answer to what "replace the
// selection" means rather than four that agree until one of them is changed.
bool killSelection() {
  u16 ln
  u16 col

  if ( !viewSelected() ) return false

  ln  = viewFromLine()
  col = viewFromCol()

  rangeDelete( ln, col, viewToLine(), viewToCol() )
  viewUnmark()
  viewGoto( ln, col )
  dirty = true
  return true
}

// The span `^C` and `^X` act on with nothing selected: the whole line and the
// newline after it, which is VS Code and is most of what either is used for. On
// the last line there is no newline to take, so it is the text alone - and that
// is also what stops a document being cut down to no lines at all.
sub lineSpan {
  spanFromLine = viewLine()
  spanFromCol  = 0

  if ( viewLine() + 1 < textLines() ) {
    spanToLine = viewLine() + 1
    spanToCol  = 0
  } else {
    spanToLine = viewLine()
    spanToCol  = lineLength( viewLine() )
  }
}

// The span the selection covers, or the line if there is none. Both `^C` and
// `^X` want exactly this, and they must agree or a cut would copy one thing and
// remove another.
sub actSpan {
  if ( viewSelected() ) {
    spanFromLine = viewFromLine()
    spanFromCol  = viewFromCol()
    spanToLine   = viewToLine()
    spanToCol    = viewToCol()
    return
  }

  lineSpan()
}

// ---- the clipboard §60 asks the program for (§37) ---------------------------
//
// **One clipboard, two places that use it.** A field with a clipboard of its own
// would agree with this one sometimes, which is worse than not having it: the
// thing a person expects is to copy in the document and paste into the find
// prompt, and that is only possible because the field asks rather than owns.
sub fieldCopyOut( u16 at, u16 n ) {
  if ( n > clipBytes ) n = clipBytes

  memCopy( addr( clip ), at, n )
  clipLen = n
}

// **Stops at the first line.** A clipboard here can hold a span of several lines
// - §61 puts a newline between them - and a field is one line. Losing the lines
// after the first is a rule a person can predict; joining them would put text on
// the line that was never copied from anywhere.
//
// The program enforces it rather than the field, because the program is what
// knows what its clipboard can contain.
u16 fieldPasteIn( u16 at, u16 max ) {
  u16 n

  n = 0
  while ( n < clipLen && n < max ) {
    if ( clip[n] == 10 || clip[n] == 13 ) break

    poke8( at + n, clip[n] )
    n++
  }

  return n
}

sub doCopy {
  actSpan()
  clipLen = rangeCopy( spanFromLine, spanFromCol, spanToLine, spanToCol,
                       addr( clip ), clipBytes )

  // A span that did not fit comes back short rather than refused, and §61 is
  // asked rather than the count compared - a span that exactly filled the
  // clipboard returns the same number as one that was cut off.
  if ( !rangeCopied() ) message = addr( sClipFull )
}

sub doCut {
  doCopy()

  // **A cut whose span did not fit the clipboard does not happen.** Cutting it
  // anyway would take out of the document what the clipboard did not keep, and
  // the only sign would be a paste that came back short later. It is §55's rule
  // about a file too big for the buffer, one level up: refuse rather than
  // truncate, and say so.
  if ( !rangeCopied() ) return

  undoStep {
    rangeDelete( spanFromLine, spanFromCol, spanToLine, spanToCol )
    viewUnmark()
    viewGoto( spanFromLine, spanFromCol )
  }

  if ( textUndoLost() ) message = addr( sNoUndo )
  dirty = true
}

sub doPaste {
  if ( clipLen == 0 ) return

  undoStep {
    killSelection()
    rangeInsert( viewLine(), viewCol(), addr( clip ), clipLen )
    viewGoto( rangeEndLine(), rangeEndCol() )
  }

  if ( textUndoLost() ) message = addr( sNoUndo )
  dirty = true
}

// The anchor goes at the top and the cursor at the end, because `viewMark`
// anchors wherever the cursor is standing.
sub doSelectAll {
  viewUnmark()
  viewGoto( 0, 0 )
  viewMark()
  viewGoto( textLines() - 1, lineLength( textLines() - 1 ) )
}

// ---- the prompt ----

// Opening one measures its own label, so a command adds a string and nothing
// else. `fieldClear` rather than a preload: with no selection, a pre-filled
// field appends to the last answer instead of replacing it.
// **A find that starts from the selection**, and selected in the field so the
// next character typed replaces it - which is what the arm below used to say it
// could not do. Its objection was exactly right and is now answered: a field
// with no selection would have appended to the term rather than replaced it,
// and §60 has a selection.
//
// Single-line selections only, and that is a boundary rather than a shortcut:
// §59's pattern is 64 bytes with no newline in it, so a span across two lines is
// not a term it could search for. One too long for the field is left alone for
// the same reason - a truncated term is a search nobody asked for.
sub prefillFromSelection {
  u16 n

  if ( !viewSelected() ) return
  if ( viewFromLine() != viewToLine() ) return

  n = viewToCol() - viewFromCol()
  if ( n == 0 || n > fieldMax ) return

  // Through §54's read interface, which is a slice rather than a character - the
  // chain is walked once for the line and not once per byte.
  n = lineSlice( viewFromLine(), viewFromCol(), n, addr( piece ) )

  fieldSet( addr( piece ), n )
  fieldSelectAll()
}

sub promptOpen( u8 what, u16 label ) {
  u16 i

  i = 0
  while ( peek8( label + i ) != '$' ) {
    i++
  }

  asking   = what
  askLabel = label
  askAt    = i
  message  = 0
  onMatch  = false

  fieldClear()
}

sub promptClose {
  asking  = askNone
  message = 0

  // A prompt that was abandoned is not on its way to quitting, whatever asked
  // for it.
  quitAfterSave = false
}

sub doGoto {
  // `std/str.momo` does the reading, because "a digit that is not one refuses the
  // whole answer" is a fact about numbers rather than about editors - and it has
  // a boundary either side of 65,535 that wants a test rather than a mirror.
  if ( !strNumber( fieldAddr(), fieldLength() ) ) {
    message = addr( sNoLine )
    return
  }

  askValue = strValue()

  if ( askValue == 0 || askValue > textLines() ) {
    message = addr( sNoLine )
    return
  }

  undoBreak()
  viewUnmark()
  viewGoto( askValue - 1, 0 )
}

// **A dirty buffer refuses to be replaced.** Opening over unsaved work is the
// same shape as saving a truncated file: one keystroke, nothing said, and the
// only copy gone. §55's rule, at the other end of the same file.
//
// **A failed open puts back what was there**, which is the half that is easy to
// miss. `loadFile` has already emptied the buffer by the time it finds out the
// file will not fit, so refusing at that point would leave an empty document
// wearing the new name - and one `^S` away from writing nothing over it. The
// buffer was not dirty to get here, so the file on disk is what it held and
// reading it again is exact.
//
// The clipboard is deliberately untouched. Cutting from one file and pasting
// into the next is most of why opening without quitting is worth having.
// **One open, and both ways in go through it.** `^O` and the panel would
// otherwise be two copies of two rules that were each bought with a real bug:
// unsaved changes are refused, and a refusal puts back the file it was showing
// because `loadFile` empties the buffer before it discovers the new one will not
// fit. A second open path gets to rediscover both.
bool openNamed( u16 at, u16 n ) {
  if ( dirty ) {
    message = addr( sDirty )
    return false
  }

  if ( n == 0 ) return false

  memCopy( addr( wasName ), addr( fileName ), len( fileName ) )
  memCopy( addr( fileName ), at, n )
  fileName[n] = 0
  haveName = true

  if ( loadFile() ) {
    viewUnmark()
    viewHome()
    dirty = false

    // **Both panes.** `^O` got away without this because the prompt it came
    // through forces a full paint on the frame after it closes; the explorer has
    // no such frame, so opening from it changed the buffer and left the previous
    // file on screen until something else happened to trigger a redraw.
    needText = true
    needExp  = true
    return true
  }

  // The name going back may be an empty one, which is the no-file startup. That
  // needs no special case: `loadFile` opens nothing, fails, and leaves the empty
  // buffer it had already made.
  message = addr( sTooBig )
  memCopy( addr( fileName ), addr( wasName ), len( fileName ) )
  haveName = fileName[0] != 0
  loadFile()
  viewUnmark()
  viewHome()
  dirty = false
  needText = true
  needExp  = true
  return false
}

sub doOpen {
  openNamed( fieldAddr(), fieldLength() )
}

// §61 does the walk, because the way it can be wrong is a span question and not
// an editor one. What is left here is what to say afterwards.
sub doReplaceAll {
  if ( findLength() == 0 ) return

  askCount = rangeReplaceAll( addr( replText ), replLen )

  if ( askCount > 0 ) {
    dirty = true
    viewUnmark()
    viewGoto( rangeEndLine(), rangeEndCol() )
  }

  message = addr( sDone )
  if ( textUndoLost() ) message = addr( sNoUndo )
}

// ---- finding ----

// **The wrap lives here and not in §59.** The library reports that it ran out of
// document; whether that means failure or starting again at the top is a policy,
// and it is the same line `undoBreak` draws - the library knows what was asked,
// the program knows what the person meant.
//
// Forward starts one past the cursor and backward stops one short of it, so a
// repeat leaves the match the cursor is already sitting on rather than finding
// it again for ever.
sub doFind( bool forward ) {
  bool got

  message = 0
  onMatch = false
  undoBreak()

  if ( findLength() == 0 ) return

  if ( forward ) {
    got = findNext( viewLine(), viewCol() + 1 )
    if ( !got ) {
      got = findNext( 0, 0 )
      if ( got ) message = addr( sWrapped )
    }
  } else {
    got = findPrev( viewLine(), viewCol() )
    if ( !got ) {
      got = findPrev( textLines() - 1, 0xFFFF )
      if ( got ) message = addr( sWrapped )
    }
  }

  if ( !got ) {
    message = addr( sNoMatch )
    return
  }

  onMatch = true
  viewGoto( findLine(), findCol() )
}

// Every key while the prompt is open, which is what makes it a mode. Up and
// down are taken before §60 sees them: a one-line field has no use for them,
// and searching backwards has no key of its own otherwise - `F3` and `Shift+F3`
// are what a person would reach for and `keyprobe` has not measured either, so
// they are not written down here on the strength of a table somebody else read.
// What Enter means, which is the only thing that differs between the five.
// **Find stays open and the rest close**, because find is the one a person
// repeats - and `askTerm` answers by asking the next question, which is how a
// two-part command gets by without a mechanism of its own.
sub promptDone {
  if ( asking == askFind ) {
    findSet( fieldAddr(), fieldLength() )
    doFind( true )
    return
  }

  if ( asking == askTerm ) {
    findSet( fieldAddr(), fieldLength() )
    promptOpen( askWith, addr( sWith ) )
    return
  }

  if ( asking == askWith ) {
    replLen = fieldLength()
    memCopy( addr( replText ), fieldAddr(), replLen )
    asking = askNone
    doReplaceAll()
    return
  }

  if ( asking == askGoto ) {
    asking = askNone
    doGoto()
    return
  }

  if ( asking == askOpen ) {
    asking = askNone
    doOpen()
    return
  }

  if ( asking == askSave ) {
    asking = askNone
    if ( fieldLength() == 0 ) return

    memCopy( addr( saveAsName ), fieldAddr(), fieldLength() )
    saveAsName[ fieldLength() ] = 0

    // **Asked before the write, because the write is what destroys it.**
    // `fileCreate` truncates the file to nothing before a byte of ours goes in,
    // so there was no point at which this could have been reported instead.
    if ( fileHere( addr( saveAsName ) ) ) {
      dlgOpen( dlgOverwrite, addr( sOverwrite ), baseName( addr( saveAsName ) ) )
      return
    }

    saveAsCommit()
  }
}

sub promptKey( u16 k ) {
  u8 r

  // Editing the answer clears what the last one was told about it. A "not
  // found" left standing over a term that has since been corrected is worse
  // than no message at all.
  message = 0

  // Up and down are the find prompt's alone. In a filename they would be two
  // keys that silently search the document behind it.
  if ( asking == askFind && ( k == keyExt + keyUp || k == keyExt + keyDown ) ) {
    findSet( fieldAddr(), fieldLength() )
    doFind( k == keyExt + keyDown )
    return
  }

  r = fieldKey( k )

  if ( r == fieldAccept ) {
    promptDone()
  } else if ( r == fieldCancel ) {
    promptClose()
  }
}

bool isMotion( u8 a ) => a == actLeft || a == actRight || a == actUp ||
                        a == actDown || a == actHome || a == actEnd ||
                        a == actTop  || a == actBottom ||
                        a == actPgUp || a == actPgDn ||
                        a == actWordL || a == actWordR

sub apply( u8 a, u16 k ) {
  // Any motion ends a run of typing. Two keystrokes with a cursor move between
  // them are two actions, and §54 cannot tell - only this knows one happened.
  if ( isMotion( a ) || a == actFind || a == actAgain ) undoBreak()

  // Everything that is not a motion may change what the text area shows, and
  // the list of those is longer and more easily added to than the list of
  // motions. One place, erring towards redrawing.
  if ( !isMotion( a ) ) needText = true

  // **Shift with a motion extends a selection and the same motion without it
  // collapses one**, which is one rule in one place rather than a shifted twin
  // of every arm below. §56 holds the anchor and has no opinion about when it
  // should move - that is a fact about the person's hand, and this is where it
  // is known.
  if ( isMotion( a ) ) {
    if ( selecting ) {
      viewMark()
    } else {
      viewUnmark()
    }
  }

  if ( a == actInsert ) {
    doInsert( u8( k ) )
  } else if ( a == actLeft ) {
    // At column 0 the left arrow goes to the end of the line above rather
    // than stopping. A key that does nothing reads as a key that is broken.
    if ( viewCol() > 0 ) {
      viewGoto( viewLine(), viewCol() - 1 )
    } else if ( viewLine() > 0 ) {
      viewGoto( viewLine() - 1, lineLength( viewLine() - 1 ) )
    }
  } else if ( a == actRight ) {
    if ( viewCol() < lineLength( viewLine() ) ) {
      viewGoto( viewLine(), viewCol() + 1 )
    } else if ( viewLine() + 1 < textLines() ) {
      viewGoto( viewLine() + 1, 0 )
    }
  } else if ( a == actUp ) {
    // `viewGotoLine` keeps the column the cursor is aiming at, so a run of
    // Down across a short line comes out where it went in.
    if ( viewLine() > 0 ) viewGotoLine( viewLine() - 1 )
  } else if ( a == actDown ) {
    viewGotoLine( viewLine() + 1 )
  } else if ( a == actHome ) {
    viewGoto( viewLine(), 0 )
  } else if ( a == actEnd ) {
    viewGoto( viewLine(), lineLength( viewLine() ) )
  } else if ( a == actPgUp ) {
    // **The window, not the screen.** These read `rows - 1` while the status line
    // was the only chrome there was, and a page that moves further than the
    // window shows is a page with lines nobody saw.
    if ( viewLine() > edHeight() ) {
      viewGotoLine( viewLine() - edHeight() )
    } else {
      viewGotoLine( 0 )
    }
  } else if ( a == actPgDn ) {
    viewGotoLine( viewLine() + edHeight() )
  } else if ( a == actBack ) {
    doBack()
  } else if ( a == actDelete ) {
    // At the end of a line Delete pulls the next one up. That is Backspace
    // from the other side and the same join, and it is what makes Delete on a
    // blank line remove the line rather than do nothing.
    if ( killSelection() ) {
      dirty = true
    } else if ( viewCol() < lineLength( viewLine() ) ) {
      lineDelete( viewLine(), viewCol() )
      dirty = true
    } else if ( viewLine() + 1 < textLines() ) {
      lineJoin( viewLine() )
      dirty = true
    }
  } else if ( a == actEnter ) {
    doEnter()
  } else if ( a == actUndo ) {
    doUndo()
  } else if ( a == actRedo ) {
    redoOnce()
    viewGoto( undoLine(), undoCol() )
    dirty = true
  } else if ( a == actTop ) {
    viewGoto( 0, 0 )
  } else if ( a == actBottom ) {
    viewGoto( textLines() - 1, 0 )
  } else if ( a == actSave ) {
    doSave()
  } else if ( a == actFind ) {
    // The field used to be emptied and left that way, because VS Code pre-fills
    // and *selects* and this had no selection to pre-fill into - so a pre-filled
    // field would have appended to the last term rather than replaced it.
    //
    // §60 has one now, so the behaviour is VS Code's: the selection if there is
    // one, selected, and empty if there is not. `^L` still repeats the last
    // search, so both behaviours exist and neither is a surprise.
    promptOpen( askFind, addr( sFind ) )
    prefillFromSelection()
  } else if ( a == actGoto ) {
    promptOpen( askGoto, addr( sGoto ) )
  } else if ( a == actOpen ) {
    promptOpen( askOpen, addr( sOpen ) )
  } else if ( a == actExplor ) {
    toggleExplorer()
  } else if ( a == actCycle ) {
    // Shift is the direction and nothing else, which is why there is one row in
    // the table rather than two.
    cycleDoc( !selecting )
  } else if ( a == actIndent ) {
    doIndent()
  } else if ( a == actNewDoc ) {
    newDoc()
  } else if ( a == actShut ) {
    closeDoc()
  } else if ( a == actSaveAs ) {
    // Straight to the question rather than through `doSave`, which only asks when
    // there is no name - and "Save As" on a named file is the case that exists
    // for.
    promptOpen( askSave, addr( sSave ) )
  } else if ( a == actQuit ) {
    tryQuit()
  } else if ( a == actPick ) {
    openPicker()
  } else if ( a == actRepl ) {
    promptOpen( askTerm, addr( sTerm ) )
    prefillFromSelection()
  } else if ( a == actWordL ) {
    // **`Ctrl+Shift+Left` needs nothing here**, and neither does its twin. §57
    // folds Shift into the key space and `step` folds it back out, so a shifted
    // Ctrl+arrow arrives as this same action with `selecting` set - and the rule
    // above has already marked the anchor by the time this runs. `keyprobe` says
    // both pairs differ in one flag bit and nothing else: `73E0` for Left and
    // `74E0` for Right, each with and without `0002`.
    wordPrev( viewLine(), viewCol() )
    viewGoto( wordLine(), wordCol() )
  } else if ( a == actWordR ) {
    wordNext( viewLine(), viewCol() )
    viewGoto( wordLine(), wordCol() )
  } else if ( a == actCopy ) {
    doCopy()
  } else if ( a == actCut ) {
    doCut()
  } else if ( a == actPaste ) {
    doPaste()
  } else if ( a == actAll ) {
    doSelectAll()
  } else if ( a == actAgain ) {
    if ( findLength() == 0 ) {
      promptOpen( askFind, addr( sFind ) )
    } else {
      doFind( true )
    }
  }
}

sub step( u16 k ) {
  u16 had
  u8  a

  // **A question owns the keyboard before anything else does.** It is the only
  // modal here that can be opened *by* another one - File > Exit opens it from a
  // menu, and Save As opens it from a prompt - so it has to be tested ahead of
  // both of the things that could have put it up.
  if ( dlgUp() ) {
    dlgKey( k )
    return
  }

  // **An open menu owns the keyboard**, ahead of the picker and the prompt alike.
  // §67 swallows whatever it does not recognise, so nothing reaches the document
  // behind it and Escape is the only way back.
  if ( menuUp() ) {
    menuStep( k )
    return
  }

  // The prompt takes everything, including `^Q` - a door out of a mode is `Esc`
  // and putting a second one in would mean a keystroke meant for the field
  // could close the program instead.
  // **The picker owns the keyboard while it is up**, ahead of the prompt,
  // because it may well have been opened from one.
  if ( picking ) {
    pickerKey( k )
    return
  }

  // `^P` is WordStar's and Borland's, where it means "take the next character
  // literally" - the nearest thing to a precedent there is, and the same job:
  // how a character the keyboard cannot send gets into the text. Ahead of the
  // prompt so that it serves both, and dead in the panel, where there is no
  // caret for the answer to land at.
  if ( k == 16 && ( prompting() || !expFocus ) ) {
    openPicker()
    return
  }

  if ( prompting() ) {
    promptKey( k )
    return
  }

  message = 0

  // §57 folds Shift into the key space; this folds it back out, so a shifted
  // arrow is the same command as the arrow with one more fact attached. That
  // keeps the binding table one row per command rather than two, and it means
  // a binding added later is reachable with Shift without being written twice.
  selecting = false
  if ( k >= keyShift ) {
    selecting = true
    k = keyExt + ( k - keyShift )
  }

  // **Before the panel, because a menu bar belongs to the screen** rather than to
  // whichever pane has focus - `Alt+F` from the explorer opens File, the same as
  // it does from the document.
  if ( menuOpened( k ) ) return

  // After the fold, so the panel sees a shifted arrow as an arrow, and before
  // everything else, because a focused panel owns the keyboard.
  if ( expFocus ) {
    explorerKey( k )
    return
  }

  // Escape with nothing to close drops the selection, which is where a hand
  // goes when it wants the highlight to stop being there.
  if ( k == keyEsc ) {
    viewUnmark()
    return
  }

  had = pending
  a = keyAction( k )

  if ( a == actNone && had == 0 && keyIsPrefix( k ) ) {
    pending = k
    return
  }

  pending = 0

  if ( k == keyQuit ) {
    tryQuit()
    return
  }

  // **The whole of Alt+numpad is this line.** The BIOS composes it and hands
  // back the finished character with a scancode of zero - `Alt+0233` measured as
  // `00E9` - so §57's key space already delivers it and the only thing in the
  // way was a range that stopped at 126.
  //
  // `keyCtrlBack` is the hole: 127 is a real glyph in codepage 437 and also what
  // `Ctrl+Backspace` reports, so a range alone would type a house where somebody
  // meant to delete a word.
  //
  // §57 already handles the other collision. `Alt+0224` is `à`, which is 0E0h,
  // which is also how a grey navigation key is flagged - and `keyNorm` tests the
  // scancode as well for exactly that reason, so a composed 0E0h with no
  // scancode comes through as the character it is.
  if ( a == actNone && had == 0 && k >= 32 && k < keyExt && k != keyCtrlBack ) {
    a = actInsert
  }

  apply( a, k )
}

// ---- entry ----

haveName = takeName()
buildPattern()

helping = fileName[1] == '?' && fileName[2] == 0 &&
          ( fileName[0] == '/' || fileName[0] == '-' )

if ( helping ) haveName = false

loud = true

// **Once, and before anything opens a file.** `textInit` lays out the machine for
// every document; `textClear` is what an open wants, and `loadFile` calls that
// one now. Its single failure is a machine with no room past the segment, and it
// has to be caught here rather than after the first `textClear` - which resets
// the flag that says so.
textInit()

bool noBuffer
noBuffer = textNoRoom()

docOpen[0] = 1

// **No arguments is a document with nowhere to go**, not an error. It used to be
// a usage line and an exit, which was right while the only way to name a file was
// on the command line - the panel is the other way, so an editor started with
// nothing now opens on the directory it is standing in.
//
// That took the usage line off the no-argument path, and with it the only thing
// tier 2 could reach: every other path here opens a screen and waits for a key,
// which under the harness is a timeout rather than a test. `/?` is where it went
// - a DOS convention worth having anyway - and `momoed.args` is how the harness
// gets to pass it.
if ( haveName ) {
  putStr( addr( sLoading ) )
  // `fileName` is nul-terminated for DOS, not `$`-terminated for the console,
  // so it cannot go through `putStr`.
  for ( u16 i = 0; fileName[i] != 0; i++ ) {
    putChar( fileName[i] )
  }

  newline()
}

if ( helping ) {
  putStr( addr( sUsage ) )
  newline()
} else if ( noBuffer || ( haveName && !loadFile() ) ) {
  // Refusing to open is the whole point: an editor that shows you part of a file
  // and then writes that part back over the whole is worse than one that will
  // not open it. **Which limit, and what this machine had**, because a file too
  // long and a machine too small want different things done next.
  newline()
  putStr( whyNotOpened() )
  newline()

  putNumber( textLines() )
  putStr( addr( sOf ) )
  putNumber( textLineRoom() )
  putStr( addr( sLines ) )

  putNumber( textRoom() )
  putStr( addr( sChunks ) )
  newline()
} else {
  loud = false

  // `videoMode` is §48's bracket over §43's save and restore, so the display
  // cannot be left in whatever this program set it to - which `simplerl` got
  // wrong for the whole of its life and paid eighty bytes to fix.
  videoMode {
    // The program owns the mode and does not set one by number. It is launched
    // into whatever the prompt left behind - `mode 80,50` gives a fifty-line
    // editor with nothing here asking for one - and imposes 80x25 only when
    // what it found cannot be described, which is a graphics mode.
    //
    // Asking for `modeText` unconditionally is what this used to do, and it
    // meant a tall screen was flattened on the way in. The mode number could not
    // say which mode 3 it was, so neither could the program.
    //
    // Any text mode the descriptor can describe, including mode 7 at 0xB000.
    // Only a graphics mode is refused now, because that is the one `adoptMode`
    // cannot answer for without §43's full query.
    if ( !adoptMode() ) setMode( modeText )

    vramSeg = screenSegment()

    // The adapter decides what an attribute means, and 0xB000 is the one that
    // has no colours. Asked of the descriptor rather than of the mode number,
    // because the segment is the thing the difference actually is.
    if ( vramSeg == 0xB000 ) {
      useMono()
    } else {
      useColor()
    }

    cols = screenWidth()
    rows = screenHeight()

    if ( cols > edMaxCols ) cols = edMaxCols
    if ( rows > edMaxRows ) rows = edMaxRows

    // **The panel is open when nothing was named**, because otherwise the screen
    // is empty and the only way forward is a key nobody has been told about. With
    // a file on the command line it stays shut: that person said what they came
    // for, and sixty-six columns is not eighty.
    expShown = !haveName
    expFocus = expShown

    // One row goes to the status line, and the window gets what the panel leaves.
    viewSize( edHeight(), edWidth() )
    viewHome()

    if ( expShown ) refreshList()

    running = true

    // **A frame nobody will see is not drawn.** With a key already waiting, the
    // screen this would paint is replaced before a person could read it, so a
    // held key costs one repaint when the hand comes off rather than one per
    // keystroke.
    //
    // This is what Page Down needed. A page changes every row and has nothing
    // to reuse, so paging is a full redraw by nature and cannot be made
    // cheaper - only rarer. `drawrate` says a full redraw is 22 a second on the
    // 286, which key repeat outruns, and that is what the beeping was.
    while ( running ) {
      if ( !keyPending() ) repaint()
      step( readKeyNorm() )
    }
  }
}
